LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l1",title:"Listening Test 1",topics:"Everyday situations · A wildlife camerawoman · Why people run · A young cellist",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman telling a friend about a cookery course.",q:"What did she like most about it?",o:["the chef's way of teaching","the recipes they cooked","meeting other people"],a:0,x:"‘What really made it for me was Marco, the chef. He never just told us what to do – he'd explain why.’ The recipes were only ‘fine’ and she didn't talk to many people.",script:[["M","So, how was the cookery course? You were really looking forward to it."],["F","It was great, actually. I thought the recipes might be a bit too ambitious for beginners, but they were fine – nothing I couldn't manage at home. And there was a nice mix of people, although to be honest I didn't really talk to many of them. What really made it for me was Marco, the chef. He never just told us what to do – he'd explain why, like why you add salt at a certain point. That's what's stuck with me."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about his journey to work.",q:"How does he feel about his new route?",o:["relieved that it is quicker","pleased that it is more interesting","annoyed that it costs more"],a:1,x:"It ‘adds about ten minutes’ (not quicker) and the fare is the same (not more expensive), but it has ‘become the best part of my day’.",script:[["M","Since they closed the bridge, I've had to go the long way round, through the old town. I was dreading it – it adds about ten minutes, and I thought I'd be stuck in traffic the whole time. But it's actually become the best part of my day. There's a market on Tuesdays, and I've started noticing all these lovely old buildings I never looked at before. The bus fare's exactly the same, so no complaints there. I might even keep going that way when the bridge reopens."]]},{ctx:"You hear two students talking about a school project.",q:"What do they agree to do?",o:["ask for more time to finish it","change the topic completely","get advice from their teacher"],a:2,x:"The boy says ‘Let's see what Mrs Evans thinks before we decide anything’ and the girl agrees: ‘Good idea.’ He rejects changing topic (‘starting from scratch’).",script:[["F","I'm starting to think we've chosen something far too big. There's just so much information about climate change."],["M","I know what you mean, but we've already done loads of research. Changing now would mean starting from scratch."],["F","True. Maybe we could just focus on one part of it – like how it affects farming?"],["M","Hmm, that's still quite broad. Let's see what Mrs Evans thinks before we decide anything."],["F","Good idea. She'll know whether it's manageable."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman leaving a phone message for a restaurant.",q:"What does she want to change?",o:["the number of people","the time of the booking","the day of the booking"],a:1,x:"‘It's still four of us’ and she mentions Saturday, but asks ‘would it be possible to make it a bit later – half past eight?’",script:[["F","Hi, this is Julia Reeves. I've got a table booked for four people on Saturday evening at eight o'clock. We're definitely still coming, and it's still four of us, but would it be possible to make it a bit later – half past eight, say? One of my friends is working until seven and she won't make it in time otherwise. If there's a problem, you can call me back on this number. Thanks very much."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a novel he has just read.",q:"What is his opinion of the book?",o:["The ending was disappointing.","The characters weren't convincing.","It was far too long."],a:0,x:"‘It's just a shame about the last chapter… everything got sorted out far too neatly.’ The characters ‘felt like real people’ and he didn't notice the length.",script:[["M","Everyone's been raving about it, so I had high expectations. And for the most part, it lived up to them. It's over six hundred pages, but I honestly didn't notice – I read it in a week. The characters felt like real people, especially the grandmother. It's just a shame about the last chapter. After all that build-up, everything got sorted out far too neatly, as if the writer had run out of ideas. It rather spoiled it for me."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about learning to drive.",q:"What does she say about her driving instructor?",o:["He was too critical of her.","He made her feel more confident.","He was often late for lessons."],a:1,x:"He pointed out mistakes ‘in a way that made me think I could fix them’, and ‘by the end I actually looked forward to lessons’. He ‘was never late’.",script:[["F","I was terrified before my first lesson – I'd heard all these stories about instructors shouting. But Ken was the calmest person I've ever met. Even when I stalled at a roundabout three times, he just said, ‘No rush, try again.’ He did point out my mistakes, obviously, but always in a way that made me think I could fix them. By the end I actually looked forward to lessons. And he was never late, not once."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about a new sports centre.",q:"What does the man think is the main problem with it?",o:["the equipment in the gym","its location","the cost of membership"],a:2,x:"‘Sixty pounds a month! … I just can't justify it.’ He praises the equipment, and the location ‘doesn't bother me’.",script:[["F","Have you been to the new sports centre yet?"],["M","Yeah, I went on Sunday. The pool's fantastic, and the gym has all the latest equipment."],["F","So you'll be joining, then?"],["M","I'd love to, but have you seen the prices? Sixty pounds a month! The old place was half that. The facilities are great, but I just can't justify it."],["F","And it's miles out of town."],["M","Well, that doesn't bother me – there's a bus stop right outside."]]},{ctx:"You hear a tour guide speaking to a group of tourists.",q:"What is the guide doing?",o:["describing the history of a building","warning the group about a danger","explaining a change to the programme"],a:2,x:"The castle is closed, ‘so instead, we'll be going to the maritime museum’. The storm is mentioned, but nobody is in danger now.",script:[["F","Right, everyone, can I have your attention for a moment? I know we were supposed to visit the castle this afternoon, but they've just closed it for repairs – part of the roof came down in last night's storm. Nobody was hurt, luckily. So instead, we'll be going to the maritime museum, which is just as interesting, I promise, and then you'll have a bit more free time in the old town before dinner."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Laura Finch talking about her work filming wildlife for television.",script:[["F",`Hello everyone, and thanks for inviting me. I'm Laura Finch, and for the last twelve years I've been filming wildlife for television documentaries. People often ask how I got started. Well, I grew up on a farm, so I was always surrounded by animals, but it was when my uncle gave me his old video camera for my thirteenth birthday that I really got hooked. I spent that whole summer filming the swallows nesting in our barn.

After university, where I studied zoology rather than film, which surprises a lot of people, I got my first job as an assistant on a series about sharks. I thought it would be glamorous, but most of my time was spent carrying heavy equipment and cleaning lenses.

The thing most people don't realise about this job is how much waiting is involved. For one series, I spent forty-three days in a hide in the Scottish Highlands, waiting for a golden eagle to return to its nest. People imagine it's the cold that's hardest, but actually it's the boredom.

Of course, there are dangerous moments too. The scariest experience I've had wasn't with a big predator, as you might expect. It was in Borneo, when a swarm of hornets attacked our camp. I was stung eleven times!

Technology has changed the job enormously. When I started, we used big, heavy cameras, but now we often use tiny ones that can be hidden inside fake rocks. Drones have also been a huge help, particularly for filming herds of animals from above without disturbing them.

My favourite project so far was a film about otters living in a city river in Norway. What made it special was that local schoolchildren helped us by recording where they'd seen the otters each day.

If you're thinking of doing this kind of work, my main piece of advice is to start small. You don't need to go to the Arctic. Film the birds in your garden or the insects in your local park, and learn everything you can about their behaviour. The best wildlife filmmakers are really naturalists first. And put your films online – that's how I got noticed.`]],items:[{s:"Laura was given her first video camera by her {g} when she was thirteen.",a:["uncle"],x:"‘my uncle gave me his old video camera for my thirteenth birthday’"},{s:"At university, Laura studied {g}.",a:["zoology"],x:"‘I studied zoology rather than film’ – film is the distractor."},{s:"In her first job, Laura mostly carried {g} and cleaned lenses.",a:["heavy equipment","equipment"],x:"‘most of my time was spent carrying heavy equipment and cleaning lenses’"},{s:"Laura once waited in a hide in Scotland for {g} days.",a:["forty-three","43","forty three"],x:"‘I spent forty-three days in a hide’"},{s:"Laura says the hardest part of waiting is not the cold but the {g}.",a:["boredom"],x:"‘People imagine it's the cold that's hardest, but actually it's the boredom.’"},{s:"Laura's most frightening experience involved {g} in Borneo.",a:["hornets","a swarm of hornets"],x:"‘It wasn't with a big predator… a swarm of hornets attacked our camp.’"},{s:"{g} are very useful for filming herds without disturbing them.",a:["drones"],x:"‘Drones have also been a huge help, particularly for filming herds of animals from above’"},{s:"Laura's favourite film was about {g} in a city river.",a:["otters"],x:"‘a film about otters living in a city river in Norway’"},{s:"Local {g} helped the team by recording where the animals were seen.",a:["schoolchildren","school children","schoolkids"],x:"‘local schoolchildren helped us by recording where they'd seen the otters’"},{s:"Laura believes the best wildlife filmmakers are {g} first.",a:["naturalists"],x:"‘The best wildlife filmmakers are really naturalists first.’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about why they started running.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about starting to run. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I was following a doctor's advice.","I wanted to spend less money on transport.","I did it to raise money for a charity.","I needed a way to cope with stress.","I was persuaded by a relative.","I wanted to prove someone wrong.","I wanted to meet new people.","I was inspired by watching a race."],speakers:[{a:4,x:"E: her brother ‘kept going on at me to join him… I gave in’. The doctor is pleased but ‘never actually suggested it’ (A).",script:[["F","I'd never been sporty at school – I was always the last one picked for teams. Then last year my brother, who's been running for ages, kept going on at me to join him on Saturday mornings. I said no about fifty times. In the end I gave in just to keep him quiet, and I'm glad I did. We run together every weekend now, and it's brought us closer. My doctor's delighted too, though she never actually suggested it."]]},{a:3,x:"D: his job is ‘high-pressure’ and running is ‘my way of dealing with a difficult day’. Saving on train fares is ‘just a bonus’ (B).",script:[["M","My job in sales is quite high-pressure, and a couple of years ago I wasn't sleeping well at all. A colleague mentioned that going for a run after work helped her switch off. I was sceptical, but I tried it, and honestly, within a week I felt calmer. It's become my way of dealing with a difficult day. I've even started running to work sometimes, which saves me a bit on train fares, but that's just a bonus."]]},{a:7,x:"H: watching people cross the marathon finish line made her think ‘I want to feel that’. Raising money came later (C).",script:[["F","A few years ago I went to watch my cousin in the city marathon. I stood at the finish line for hours, and seeing all these ordinary people – some of them in their seventies – crossing the line in tears, I just thought, ‘I want to feel that.’ I signed up for my first race the next week. I've done three marathons since then, and I've raised money for a children's hospital along the way, which is lovely."]]},{a:6,x:"G: he ‘didn't know a soul’ and joined a club; ‘most of my closest friends here are people I met through the club’.",script:[["M","When I moved to a new city for work, I didn't know a soul, and evenings in my flat were pretty lonely. I saw a poster for a running club that met on Thursdays, and even though I wasn't particularly fit, I went along. Everyone was really welcoming, and we always go for a meal afterwards. Most of my closest friends here are people I met through the club. The running is almost secondary!"]]},{a:5,x:"F: her ex-boyfriend said she'd never have the discipline; she ran a half marathon and sent him the photo.",script:[["F","My ex-boyfriend once told me I'd never have the discipline to run even five kilometres. He said it as a joke, but it really annoyed me. So the day after we broke up, I downloaded a training app and started. Six months later I ran a half marathon, and I sent him the photo of me at the finish line. Childish, maybe, but very satisfying."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a young musician called Ben Carter, who plays the cello in an orchestra.",script:[["F","My guest today is Ben Carter, who at just twenty-two has become one of the youngest members of the National Symphony Orchestra. Ben, welcome."],["M","Thanks for having me."],["F","You started playing the cello quite late, didn't you?"],["M","Yes, I was twelve, which is late for a classical musician. Most of my colleagues started when they were four or five. Actually, I wanted to play the drums, but my parents said our flat was too small and the neighbours would complain. The school had a spare cello nobody was using, so my music teacher suggested I give it a try. I wasn't keen at first, to be honest – it seemed a bit old-fashioned."],["F","So what changed your mind?"],["M","It was one particular piece of music. My teacher played me a recording of Bach's cello suites, and something about the sound – it's so close to a human voice – just grabbed me. I went home and practised for three hours. My parents couldn't believe it, because I'd never stuck at anything before."],["F","Getting into a professional orchestra is extremely competitive. How did you prepare for the audition?"],["M","The playing itself I'd been preparing for years. What I hadn't prepared for was the nerves. In the first audition I did, for a different orchestra, my hands were shaking so much that I couldn't control the bow, and I didn't get in. So before the next one, I performed in front of people as often as I possibly could – in care homes, at train stations, anywhere. By the time the real audition came, playing to a panel didn't feel so strange."],["F","What's life in an orchestra actually like?"],["M","People imagine it's all glamorous concerts, but there's a huge amount of rehearsal – often six hours a day – and a lot of travel. The hardest adjustment for me, though, was that you're no longer an individual. As a student, I was used to deciding how I wanted to play a piece. In an orchestra, the conductor decides, and you have to blend in with sixty other people. It took me a while to accept that."],["F","You've also become quite well known online."],["M","That was an accident, really. A friend filmed me playing a pop song on the cello in the street and put it on social media, and it got two million views in a week. Some people in the classical world were a bit sniffy about it, but I think it's brilliant if it gets young people interested. I get messages from kids saying they've started lessons because of my videos, and that means far more to me than the number of views."],["F","Do you ever find time to relax?"],["M","Not as much as I'd like. When I do, I go climbing. My colleagues think I'm mad – if I injured my hands, I couldn't play. But I'm careful, and I need something that gets me completely out of my head. When you're halfway up a rock face, you can't think about rehearsals or anything else."],["F","And finally, what's next for you?"],["M","I'd love to try composing. I've written a few short pieces, nothing serious yet. Eventually I'd like to write music for films. But I'm in no hurry – I've got a lot to learn from the orchestra first."]],qs:[{q:"Why did Ben start playing the cello?",o:["His parents wanted him to learn a classical instrument.","He wasn't able to play the instrument he wanted.","He was inspired by a performance at school."],a:1,x:"He wanted the drums, but the flat was too small, so his teacher suggested the spare cello."},{q:"What made Ben enthusiastic about the cello?",o:["the sound of a piece of music","his teacher's praise","his parents' reaction"],a:0,x:"Bach's cello suites – ‘something about the sound… just grabbed me’."},{q:"What did Ben do differently before his successful audition?",o:["He practised the pieces for longer.","He got used to playing in front of people.","He chose easier music to play."],a:1,x:"He ‘performed in front of people as often as I possibly could’ to deal with nerves."},{q:"What did Ben find hardest when he joined the orchestra?",o:["the long rehearsals","travelling so often","not being able to play in his own way"],a:2,x:"‘The hardest adjustment… the conductor decides, and you have to blend in.’"},{q:"How does Ben feel about his online popularity?",o:["proud that it has encouraged others to play","worried that it has damaged his reputation","surprised that other musicians approve of it"],a:0,x:"Messages from kids who started lessons ‘mean far more to me than the number of views’."},{q:"Why does Ben go climbing?",o:["to keep his hands strong","because his colleagues recommended it","because it stops him thinking about work"],a:2,x:"‘I need something that gets me completely out of my head… you can't think about rehearsals.’"},{q:"What does Ben say about his future?",o:["He plans to leave the orchestra soon.","He wants more experience before composing seriously.","He has already written music for a film."],a:1,x:"‘I'm in no hurry – I've got a lot to learn from the orchestra first.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l2",title:"Listening Test 2",topics:"Everyday situations · A turtle volunteer · Moving home · The science of happiness",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a man telling a friend about his new phone.",q:"What does he dislike about it?",o:["the battery life","the size of the screen","the cost of extras"],a:2,x:"‘What really gets me is that nothing's included any more… the case they recommend costs almost as much as a meal out.’ The battery ‘easily gets me through the day’ and he got used to the screen.",script:[["F","So, are you happy with the new phone?"],["M","Mostly, yes. The camera's amazing, and I was worried the battery wouldn't last, but it easily gets me through the day. The screen's a bit smaller than my old one, but I got used to that in about a week. What really gets me is that nothing's included any more. No charger, no headphones, and the case they recommend costs almost as much as a meal out. It feels like they're making money out of every little thing."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a course she did.",q:"Why did she decide to do the course?",o:["Her employer asked her to.","She was thinking of changing career.","A friend recommended it."],a:1,x:"‘I'd been thinking about leaving my job in marketing… I wanted to see whether I could actually earn a living taking photos.’ She paid herself, and her friend thought she was crazy.",script:[["F","People assume my company sent me on the photography course, because I use a camera at work sometimes, but actually I paid for it myself. I'd been thinking about leaving my job in marketing for a while, and I wanted to see whether I could actually earn a living taking photos. My friend Sarah thought I was crazy – she said I should just keep it as a hobby. Anyway, it went well, and I've got my first paid job next month."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a restaurant.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The service was slow.","The food was good value.","There weren't enough choices on the menu."],a:1,x:"He says it was ‘really reasonable for the quality’ and she replies ‘Oh, definitely.’ They disagree about the waiting time and the vegetarian options.",script:[["F","I thought the food was great, but we waited forever between courses."],["M","Did we? I didn't really notice – we were chatting. What I did think was that it was really reasonable for the quality."],["F","Oh, definitely. I've paid twice as much for worse food."],["M","Me too. I'd have liked a few more vegetarian options, though."],["F","Really? I thought there was plenty."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a phone message for a colleague.",q:"What does he want her to do?",o:["email him a document","change the time of a meeting","book a meeting room"],a:0,x:"‘Could you email me the report I left on my desk?’ He doesn't want to move the meeting, and the room is already booked.",script:[["M","Hi Laura, it's Paul. I'm stuck on the train – there's a problem with the signals, so I'm going to be at least an hour late. The meeting with the clients is at ten, and I really don't want to move it again, so could you email me the report I left on my desk? Then I can read it on the way and be ready as soon as I arrive. The room's already booked, so don't worry about that. Thanks."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a holiday.",q:"How did she feel at the end of the holiday?",o:["sorry to be going home","disappointed by the weather","relieved to be leaving"],a:0,x:"‘When the taxi came to take us to the airport, I could have cried. I'm already looking at dates to go back.’ The rain they expected never came.",script:[["F","We'd been told it would rain the whole week, so we packed umbrellas and waterproofs, and in the end we didn't need any of them. The hotel was lovely and the town was so friendly. By the last day I'd completely switched off from work, and honestly, when the taxi came to take us to the airport, I could have cried. I'm already looking at dates to go back."]]},{ctx:"You hear a student talking about a school science project.",q:"What was the most difficult part of the project?",o:["collecting the data","working in a team","writing the report"],a:2,x:"‘The nightmare was putting it all into the final report… We rewrote it about five times.’ Getting data was ‘fun’ and the group ‘got on really well’.",script:[["M","Our project was on air quality around the school. Getting the data was actually fun – we had sensors all over the place, and everyone wanted to help. And the four of us in the group got on really well, which isn't always the case. The nightmare was putting it all into the final report. We had so much information, and we had to fit it into two thousand words. We rewrote it about five times."]]},{ctx:"You hear a gardening expert speaking on the radio.",q:"What is she doing?",o:["explaining why a method is popular","warning listeners about a mistake","describing her own garden"],a:1,x:"Watering a little every day ‘feels like the caring thing to do. But it actually keeps the roots near the surface’ – she warns against it and gives better advice.",script:[["F","A lot of you have written in about your tomato plants this summer, and I think I know what's going on. Many people are watering them a little every day, which feels like the caring thing to do. But it actually keeps the roots near the surface, and the plants suffer as soon as it gets hot. Water them deeply twice a week instead, and you'll see a real difference."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about learning to swim as an adult.",q:"What does he say about his swimming teacher?",o:["She made the lessons fun.","She was very strict.","She understood his fears."],a:2,x:"‘She'd clearly worked with plenty of nervous adults, and she never once pushed me to do something before I was ready.’ The lessons were serious, not fun (A), but she wasn't strict (B).",script:[["M","I was forty-two and couldn't swim, and I was so embarrassed about it that I nearly didn't turn up. The teacher, Carol, wasn't exactly a barrel of laughs – the lessons were serious and there was a lot of repetition. But she'd clearly worked with plenty of nervous adults, and she never once pushed me to do something before I was ready. I don't think I'd have kept going with anyone else."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a man called Marcus Webb talking about volunteering on a sea turtle project in Costa Rica.",script:[["M",`Hi everyone. I'm Marcus, and last year I spent four months volunteering on a sea turtle project on the Pacific coast of Costa Rica. I'd been working in an office in Manchester for six years, and I wanted to do something completely different before I turned thirty.

The project I joined protects the nests of olive ridley turtles. Every night, groups of volunteers walked along an eight-kilometre stretch of beach, looking for female turtles coming ashore to lay their eggs. We couldn't use normal torches, because bright light confuses the turtles, so we used red lights instead, which the turtles can't really see.

When we found a nest, we carefully collected the eggs and moved them to a protected area called a hatchery. That sounds strange, but the main threat to the eggs isn't other animals – it's poachers, people who dig up the eggs to sell them. In the hatchery, the eggs were guarded twenty-four hours a day.

The eggs take about forty-five days to hatch. My favourite job was releasing the baby turtles. We'd wait until the evening, when it was cooler, and let them walk down to the sea by themselves. It's important that they make that journey on their own, because it helps them remember the beach and return there as adults.

Life at the camp was basic. We slept in wooden huts, and there was electricity for only three hours a day, so I read more books in those four months than in the previous five years. The biggest challenge for me, though, wasn't the lack of comfort – it was the mosquitoes. I've never been bitten so much in my life.

Most volunteers were in their twenties, but there was a retired teacher from Canada who was seventy-one and could walk further than any of us. Everyone paid a weekly fee to cover food and accommodation, which surprised some people, but it also pays the local staff who run the project all year round.

If you're thinking of doing something similar, my advice is to check that the project works closely with the local community. The best projects employ local people and support schools in the area, so that protecting the turtles benefits everyone.

Since I came back, I've changed jobs, and I now work for an environmental charity. So in a way, the turtles changed my life as much as I helped theirs.`]],items:[{s:"Before the trip, Marcus had worked in an {g} for six years.",a:["office"],x:"‘I'd been working in an office in Manchester for six years.’"},{s:"Volunteers patrolled a beach that was {g} kilometres long.",a:["eight","8"],x:"‘an eight-kilometre stretch of beach’"},{s:"Volunteers used {g} lights so that the turtles wouldn't be confused.",a:["red"],x:"‘We couldn't use normal torches… so we used red lights instead.’"},{s:"Eggs were moved to a protected area known as a {g}.",a:["hatchery"],x:"‘a protected area called a hatchery’"},{s:"The main threat to the eggs comes from {g}.",a:["poachers"],x:"‘The main threat… isn't other animals – it's poachers.’"},{s:"The eggs hatch after about {g} days.",a:["forty-five","45","forty five"],x:"‘The eggs take about forty-five days to hatch.’"},{s:"Baby turtles were released in the {g}, when it was cooler.",a:["evening"],x:"‘We'd wait until the evening, when it was cooler.’"},{s:"At the camp there was electricity for {g} hours a day.",a:["three","3"],x:"‘electricity for only three hours a day’"},{s:"Marcus found the {g} the biggest challenge of camp life.",a:["mosquitoes","mosquitos"],x:"‘The biggest challenge… wasn't the lack of comfort – it was the mosquitoes.’"},{s:"Marcus now works for an {g}.",a:["environmental charity"],x:"‘I now work for an environmental charity.’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about moving to a new home.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about their move. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["It took longer than I expected to feel at home.","The neighbours have been very welcoming.","I miss the place where I used to live.","It has improved my relationship with my family.","I underestimated how much the move would cost.","I've taken up a new activity since moving.","I had no choice about moving.","We made the decision too quickly."],speakers:[{a:0,x:"A: ‘for the first six months I felt like a tourist rather than someone who lived there… It was only around Christmas… that it started to feel like home.’",script:[["F","We moved to the coast last spring, and I'd imagined it would all be wonderful straight away. In reality, for the first six months I felt like a tourist rather than someone who lived there. I didn't know where anything was, and I kept driving back to the city at weekends to see old friends. It was only around Christmas, when I realised I knew the names of half the people in the local café, that it started to feel like home."]]},{a:4,x:"E: ‘By the end we'd spent nearly twice what we'd planned.’ The neighbours are only ‘nice enough’ (B).",script:[["M","Honestly, the removal company's price was just the start. Nobody warns you about all the extra bits – new curtains because the windows are a different size, a washing machine because the old one wouldn't fit, paint, a new sofa. By the end we'd spent nearly twice what we'd planned. The house itself is great, and the neighbours seem nice enough, but it's going to be a while before we can afford a holiday."]]},{a:5,x:"F: ‘I've started running every morning, which I'd never have done before.’ They chose the flat themselves, so G doesn't fit.",script:[["F","We chose this flat mainly because it was cheaper, and I wasn't expecting much from the area. But it's right next to a huge park, and I've started running every morning, which I'd never have done before. I've even entered a half marathon. So a move that was really just about saving money has turned out to be good for me in a completely different way."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘I've got to know my dad much better now that we talk as adults.’",script:[["M","I moved back in with my parents after university to save money, and I was dreading it. I thought we'd argue all the time, like when I was a teenager. But it's actually been really nice. We cook together most evenings, and I've got to know my dad much better now that we talk as adults. I'll move out eventually, but I'm in no hurry."]]},{a:7,x:"H: ‘Looking back, we should have taken more time… if we'd waited a few weeks, I think we'd have chosen differently.’",script:[["F","We saw the house on a Saturday and made an offer on the Monday, because the estate agent said there were lots of other buyers. Looking back, we should have taken more time. We never checked how noisy the road is at rush hour, and we didn't realise how far it was from the children's school. It's not terrible, but if we'd waited a few weeks, I think we'd have chosen differently."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a psychologist called Dr Nadia Osei, who researches happiness.",script:[["M","My guest today is Dr Nadia Osei, a psychologist who researches happiness. Nadia, welcome."],["F","Thank you."],["M","How did you end up studying happiness? It's not an obvious career."],["F","No, it wasn't planned at all. I trained as a clinical psychologist, so I spent years working with people who were depressed or anxious. What I noticed was that when they got better, we knew very little about how to help them actually flourish, rather than just stop suffering. That gap is what pulled me into research."],["M","One of your best-known studies is about money. What did you find?"],["F","People often expect me to say that money doesn't make you happy, but that's not quite true. Having enough to live without constant worry makes a big difference. Beyond that point, though, earning more has surprisingly little effect. What seems to matter more is how you spend it. People who spend money on experiences, or on other people, report more lasting happiness than those who buy things for themselves."],["M","Is happiness something we can change, or are we born with a certain level?"],["F","Genes do play a part – some people are naturally more cheerful. But research suggests that a large proportion is influenced by our habits and choices. I'm always careful here, though, because I don't want people to think that being unhappy is their own fault. Things like illness, poverty or loneliness have a real impact, and you can't simply think your way out of those."],["M","Which habits make the biggest difference?"],["F","If I had to pick one, it would be relationships. The strongest predictor of wellbeing in almost every study is the quality of people's connections with others. It doesn't need to be a huge circle of friends – a few close relationships are enough. Exercise and sleep help too, of course, but they come second."],["M","What about social media? Does it make us unhappy?"],["F","It's more complicated than the headlines suggest. Using it to stay in touch with people you care about can be positive. The problem is passive scrolling – looking at other people's carefully edited lives and comparing yourself with them. In our studies, it wasn't the amount of time people spent online that predicted a lower mood, but what they were actually doing while they were there."],["M","Do you apply your research to your own life?"],["F","I try to, but I'm not a perfect example. I work too much, like most academics. What I have changed is that I now keep my weekends free of work, and I make a point of seeing friends every week, even when I'm busy. I used to cancel plans whenever a deadline came up. I don't do that any more."],["M","And finally, what would you say to someone who wants to feel happier?"],["F","Don't aim to be happy all the time. That's not realistic, and people who try usually end up disappointed. Feeling sad or frustrated sometimes is a normal part of life. Focus instead on doing things that feel meaningful to you, and happiness tends to follow."]],qs:[{q:"What led Nadia to start researching happiness?",o:["A colleague suggested it to her.","She was unhappy in her previous job.","She noticed a gap in what was known."],a:2,x:"‘We knew very little about how to help them actually flourish… That gap is what pulled me into research.’"},{q:"What does Nadia say about money and happiness?",o:["Money has no effect on how happy people are.","How people spend money matters more than how much they earn.","People who earn more are always happier."],a:1,x:"‘Beyond that point… earning more has surprisingly little effect. What seems to matter more is how you spend it.’"},{q:"Why is Nadia careful when she talks about changing how happy we are?",o:["She doesn't want unhappy people to blame themselves.","The research on genes is still uncertain.","She thinks people exaggerate the effect of habits."],a:0,x:"‘I don't want people to think that being unhappy is their own fault.’"},{q:"According to Nadia, what has the greatest effect on wellbeing?",o:["having close relationships","getting enough sleep","having a large group of friends"],a:0,x:"‘The strongest predictor… is the quality of people's connections… a few close relationships are enough.’ Sleep comes ‘second’."},{q:"What does Nadia say about social media?",o:["People should spend less time online.","It has been proved to damage people's mood.","What people do online matters more than how long they spend."],a:2,x:"‘It wasn't the amount of time people spent online… but what they were actually doing.’"},{q:"What change has Nadia made in her own life?",o:["She has reduced her working hours.","She no longer cancels plans with friends.","She has started exercising every week."],a:1,x:"‘I used to cancel plans whenever a deadline came up. I don't do that any more.’ She still works too much (A)."},{q:"What is Nadia's final advice?",o:["Try to stay positive in every situation.","Set clear goals for your happiness.","Accept that negative feelings are normal."],a:2,x:"‘Don't aim to be happy all the time… Feeling sad or frustrated sometimes is a normal part of life.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l3",title:"Listening Test 3",topics:"Everyday situations · A beekeeper · Moving to a new city · A mountain photographer",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman telling a friend about a film she saw.",q:"What did she think of it?",o:["The story was hard to follow.","It was too long.","The acting was disappointing."],a:1,x:"‘Three hours? They could have cut forty minutes easily.’ She praises the actors and says she had no trouble following the story.",script:[["F","Everyone said it was brilliant, and to be fair the actors were fantastic – especially the girl who played the daughter. And I didn't have any trouble following what was going on, even with all the jumps backwards and forwards in time. But honestly, three hours? They could have cut forty minutes easily. By the end I just wanted to go home."],["M","So you wouldn't recommend it?"],["F","I would, but only to people with a lot of patience."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about learning to cook.",q:"Why did he start taking cookery lessons?",o:["to save money","to impress his partner","to eat more healthily"],a:2,x:"After a check-up his cholesterol was high and he had been ‘living on ready meals for years’. He rejects the other two reasons.",script:[["M","People always assume I did it to impress my girlfriend, but she's a much better cook than me and she was happy doing it. And cooking from scratch isn't really cheaper, not with the ingredients I like. No, it was after a check-up at the doctor's. My cholesterol was high, and I realised I'd been living on ready meals for years. That was the push I needed."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a new sports centre.",q:"What does the woman complain about?",o:["the opening hours","the price of membership","the lack of parking"],a:0,x:"‘It doesn't open until nine, which is no good if you want to swim before work.’ She thinks the price is reasonable and it's next to the car park.",script:[["F","Have you been to the new sports centre yet?"],["M","Once. It's pretty expensive, though."],["F","Is it? I thought it was reasonable for what you get – there's a pool and two gyms. And it's right next to the car park, which is great. My only problem is that it doesn't open until nine, which is no good if you want to swim before work."],["M","That's a shame."]]},{ctx:"You hear a teacher talking to a student.",q:"What is the teacher doing?",o:["encouraging the student to enter a competition","warning the student about a deadline","suggesting the student changes a story"],a:0,x:"‘I think you should send it in. You wouldn't need to change a thing.’ The closing date is a month away, so it isn't a warning.",script:[["F","Jamie, have you got a minute? I've just read your short story – the one about the lighthouse. It's really good. There's a writing competition for young people run by the local library, and the closing date's not until the end of next month, so there's plenty of time. I think you should send it in. You wouldn't need to change a thing."],["M","Really? I wasn't sure it was good enough."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a voicemail message.",q:"Why is he calling?",o:["to change an arrangement","to apologise for missing something","to ask for directions"],a:1,x:"‘I'm so sorry about last night. I completely forgot we'd arranged to have dinner.’ Arranging another evening is for later.",script:[["M","Hi Sophie, it's Dan. I'm so sorry about last night. I completely forgot we'd arranged to have dinner – I only remembered this morning when I saw your messages. I know you waited for ages. There's no excuse, really. Let me make it up to you – I'll call you later and we can sort out another evening, if you're still speaking to me!"]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about her job as a tour guide.",q:"What does she find most difficult about the job?",o:["remembering historical facts","dealing with bad weather","keeping groups together"],a:2,x:"‘What really wears me out is making sure nobody wanders off.’ Facts ‘stick in your head’ and rain doesn't bother her.",script:[["F","People think the hard part must be learning all the dates and names, but after a few weeks they just stick in your head. And rain doesn't bother me – I've got a good umbrella, and tourists expect it in London anyway. What really wears me out is making sure nobody wanders off. You turn round to point at a building, and suddenly two people have disappeared into a souvenir shop."]]},{ctx:"You hear two students talking about a presentation.",q:"What do they decide to do?",o:["practise together at the weekend","divide the work between them","ask their tutor for more time"],a:1,x:"‘I think we should just split it’ – ‘OK, that makes sense.’ The tutor has refused extensions and she is working all weekend.",script:[["M","We've only got a week. Should we ask Dr Hall for an extension?"],["F","She's already said no to everyone else. I think we should just split it – you do the history part and I'll do the statistics."],["M","OK, that makes sense. And we could meet on Saturday to run through it?"],["F","I'm working all weekend, sorry. Let's just send each other our slides."],["M","Fine. Deal."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a holiday.",q:"How did he feel about the hotel?",o:["It was better than he had expected.","It was not as described online.","It was too far from the beach."],a:0,x:"‘I wasn't expecting much. But the room was huge…’ It was five minutes from the beach, ‘like the website said’.",script:[["M","I'd booked it at the last minute, and the reviews were pretty mixed, so I wasn't expecting much. But the room was huge, the staff couldn't do enough for us, and it was exactly five minutes' walk to the beach, like the website said. Honestly, we'd happily go back."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Chloe Barnes talking about her work as a beekeeper.",script:[["F",`Hello everyone. I'm Chloe, and I've been keeping bees for about twelve years now. I actually started when I was working as an accountant – a friend gave me a hive as a birthday present, which I thought was a very strange gift at the time.

Today I look after sixty hives on farms around the county. A strong hive in summer can contain around fifty thousand bees, although that number drops a lot in winter.

People often ask me whether I get stung. The answer is yes, several times a week, but I'm used to it. The thing I really worry about isn't stings, it's the weather. A long, wet spring is the worst thing for bees, because they can't fly out to collect food.

Most of my honey is sold at farmers' markets, but I also supply a small bakery in the town centre, which uses it in their bread.

The flavour of honey depends completely on the flowers the bees visit. Honey from bees near lavender fields, for example, is quite different from honey made near apple orchards. My personal favourite is heather honey, which is dark and quite strong.

One thing I'd love people to understand is how important bees are for food production. Roughly a third of the food we eat depends on insects like bees for pollination.

If you want to help, you don't need to keep bees yourself. The simplest thing is to plant flowers, and to avoid chemical sprays in your garden. And if you do want to become a beekeeper, my advice is to join a local club first. You'll learn far more from experienced keepers than you ever will from a book.`]],items:[{s:"Chloe was working as an {g} when she started keeping bees.",a:["accountant"],x:"‘I actually started when I was working as an accountant.’"},{s:"Her first hive was a {g} from a friend.",a:["birthday present","present","gift","birthday gift"],x:"‘a friend gave me a hive as a birthday present’."},{s:"She now looks after {g} hives.",a:["sixty","60"],x:"‘Today I look after sixty hives.’"},{s:"In summer, a strong hive can contain about {g} bees.",a:["fifty thousand","50,000","50000","50 000"],x:"‘around fifty thousand bees’."},{s:"Chloe worries more about the {g} than about being stung.",a:["weather"],x:"‘The thing I really worry about isn't stings, it's the weather.’"},{s:"As well as selling at markets, she supplies honey to a {g}.",a:["bakery","small bakery"],x:"‘I also supply a small bakery in the town centre’."},{s:"Honey made near lavender tastes different from honey made near {g}.",a:["apple orchards","orchards"],x:"‘quite different from honey made near apple orchards’."},{s:"Chloe's favourite kind of honey is {g} honey.",a:["heather"],x:"‘My personal favourite is heather honey.’"},{s:"About {g} of our food depends on pollination by insects.",a:["a third","one third","1/3"],x:"‘Roughly a third of the food we eat depends on insects.’"},{s:"She advises new beekeepers to join a {g}.",a:["local club","club","beekeeping club"],x:"‘my advice is to join a local club first’."}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about moving to a new city.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about the experience. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I found it hard to make friends at first.","I was surprised by how expensive everything was.","I regret not moving sooner.","I miss the slower pace of life where I grew up.","I moved because of a relationship.","I got lost more often than I expected.","I had to learn a new language quickly.","I found work more easily than I expected."],speakers:[{a:2,x:"C: ‘Why on earth did I wait so long? I'd be so much further on if I'd done it at twenty-five.’",script:[["M","I spent years talking about leaving my hometown and moving to Manchester, and I kept finding reasons not to – my job, my flat, my football team. When I finally did it, at thirty-two, everything just fell into place. I found a flat near the centre and joined a running club in my first week. My only thought now is: why on earth did I wait so long? I'd be so much further on if I'd done it at twenty-five."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘I find the rush exhausting… I realise how much I've missed just slowing down.’ She says the cost isn't as bad as people warned (not B).",script:[["F","I grew up in a tiny village in Wales, and everyone told me I'd love London. And in lots of ways I do – there's always something on, and the cost isn't as bad as people warned me, because I share a flat. But I find the rush exhausting. Everyone's walking fast; nobody has time for a chat in the shop. When I go home to visit, I realise how much I've missed just slowing down."]]},{a:0,x:"A: ‘What I hadn't anticipated was the loneliness… I mostly ate dinner alone.’ His job was already arranged, so H doesn't fit.",script:[["M","I moved to Edinburgh because I already had a job lined up at the university. What I hadn't anticipated was the loneliness. My colleagues were friendly at work, but everyone had their own lives in the evenings. For the first six months I mostly ate dinner alone in front of the TV. It was only when I joined a choir that things began to change."]]},{a:4,x:"E: ‘My boyfriend got a place on a medical course in Lyon… I'd never have considered living abroad if it hadn't been for him.’ She already spoke some French (not G).",script:[["F","My boyfriend got a place on a medical course in Lyon, and I didn't want us to live apart for five years, so I went too. I'd studied French at school, so I could get by, although it took a while before people stopped switching to English. The city's beautiful, and we've got a lovely group of friends now. Honestly, I'd never have considered living abroad if it hadn't been for him."]]},{a:5,x:"F: ‘for the first month I'd regularly come out on completely the wrong side of the station.’ Food was cheaper than expected (not B).",script:[["M","Tokyo is the most organised city I've ever been in, but that didn't stop me constantly ending up in the wrong place. The train stations are enormous – some have more than two hundred exits – and for the first month I'd regularly come out on completely the wrong side of the station. Everyone warned me about the prices, but actually food is cheaper than back home in London. I just wish someone had warned me about the maps!"]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a man called Ravi Mehta, who is a professional mountain photographer.",script:[["F","My guest today is Ravi Mehta, whose photographs of the Himalayas have appeared in magazines all over the world. Ravi, how did you get started?"],["M","Almost by accident, really. I was a keen climber long before I was a photographer. I used to take a cheap camera on expeditions just to show my family where I'd been. Then a friend who worked for a travel magazine saw some of my pictures and asked if she could use a couple. I didn't even know you could get paid for that."],["F","What's the hardest thing about taking photographs in the mountains?"],["M","People expect me to say the cold, and it's true that batteries die very quickly at high altitude – I keep spares inside my jacket. But you get used to that. The real challenge is patience. You might climb for six hours to reach a viewpoint, and then the cloud comes in and you see nothing. Sometimes I wait two or three days for the right light."],["F","Do you ever take risks to get a picture?"],["M","When I was younger, definitely. There was one time I walked out onto a ridge in bad weather because I was sure the shot would be spectacular. It was, but I also nearly fell. My guide was furious with me, and he was right. These days, no photograph is worth that. If the conditions are dangerous, I turn back."],["F","How has technology changed your work?"],["M","Drones have made a huge difference. I can now get angles that would once have needed a helicopter. But I'm a bit uncomfortable about how they're used. In some national parks there are so many drones buzzing around that they disturb the wildlife – and the other visitors. I only fly mine where it's permitted and when there's nobody else around."],["F","You also run photography trips for amateurs. What do people find most surprising?"],["M","How little the camera matters. People turn up with enormous, expensive lenses, and then they're amazed when someone with a phone takes the best picture of the day. It's all about noticing – where the light is falling, what's in the foreground. I spend the first morning of every trip without cameras at all, just looking."],["F","Is there a photograph you're proudest of?"],["M","Not one of the famous ones, actually. It's a picture of an old woman in a village in Nepal, holding a cup of tea and laughing at something my guide had said. It's not technically perfect – it's slightly out of focus – but whenever I look at it, I remember the whole day. That's what a good photograph should do."],["F","And what's next for you?"],["M","I've spent twenty years photographing the highest mountains in the world. Now I want to do the opposite. I'm starting a project about the small hills near where I grew up, in the north of England. People travel thousands of miles for a view and forget what's on their doorstep."]],qs:[{q:"Ravi became a professional photographer because",o:["he had studied photography at college.","someone asked to publish his pictures.","he wanted to show his family his climbs."],a:1,x:"A friend at a travel magazine ‘asked if she could use a couple’. Showing his family was why he took photos, not why he became a professional."},{q:"According to Ravi, the biggest challenge in his work is",o:["waiting for the right conditions.","keeping his equipment working in the cold.","carrying heavy equipment to viewpoints."],a:0,x:"‘The real challenge is patience… Sometimes I wait two or three days for the right light.’"},{q:"What does Ravi say about taking risks?",o:["He still thinks some risks are worth taking.","He now regards an earlier decision as a mistake.","His guide encouraged him to be more adventurous."],a:1,x:"He nearly fell; ‘My guide was furious with me, and he was right. These days, no photograph is worth that.’"},{q:"How does Ravi feel about drones?",o:["They are too expensive for most photographers.","They have made his work less interesting.","They can cause problems when used carelessly."],a:2,x:"Too many drones ‘disturb the wildlife – and the other visitors’."},{q:"What surprises people on Ravi's photography trips?",o:["Expensive equipment is not essential.","They have to get up very early.","The landscapes are very varied."],a:0,x:"‘How little the camera matters… someone with a phone takes the best picture of the day.’"},{q:"Why is Ravi proudest of the photo of the old woman?",o:["It was technically the hardest shot to take.","It brings back strong memories.","It made him well known."],a:1,x:"‘whenever I look at it, I remember the whole day’ – it isn't technically perfect or famous."},{q:"What is Ravi's next project?",o:["photographing mountains he has never climbed","returning to the Himalayas with new equipment","taking pictures of places near where he grew up"],a:2,x:"‘a project about the small hills near where I grew up’."}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l4",title:"Listening Test 4",topics:"Everyday situations · A city beekeeper · Why people learned to cook · A mountain rescue volunteer",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a bicycle she has bought.",q:"What does she say about it?",o:["It was cheaper than she expected.","It is heavier than her old one.","It needed repairing straight away."],a:1,x:"‘the frame weighs a good deal more than my old one’. The price was ‘more than I meant to spend’ and nothing needed repairing – the brakes were ‘adjusted before I collected it’.",script:[["F","I finally replaced that old bike of mine. It was more than I meant to spend, but the shop adjusted the brakes and everything before I collected it, so it was ready to ride. The only thing I hadn't thought about is that the frame weighs a good deal more than my old one. Getting it up the stairs to the flat is quite a workout. On the road you don't notice at all, mind you."]]},{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about a meeting.",q:"What do they agree about it?",o:["It started too late.","It covered too many topics.","It should have been an email."],a:1,x:"‘we jumped from budgets to the website to the summer party’ and he agrees ‘far too much for an hour’. Starting late is mentioned but dismissed; the email idea is the woman's joke, which he doesn't accept.",script:[["M","Well, that was two hours of my life."],["F","It only overran by ten minutes, to be fair. What got me was that we jumped from budgets to the website to the summer party."],["M","You're right, that was the problem – far too much for an hour. Nobody could follow it."],["F","It could all have been an email."],["M","Some of it, maybe, but the budget bit did need talking through."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a message about a delivery.",q:"What is he asking the company to do?",o:["deliver the item on a different day","leave the item with a neighbour","refund the delivery charge"],a:0,x:"‘could you change it to Thursday?’ He says he doesn't want it left next door, and he doesn't mention money.",script:[["M","Hello, this is Adam Price, order 4-4-1-9. You're due to deliver my table tomorrow, but I've just been told I have to be at work all day. I'd rather you didn't leave it next door, because it's far too big for their hallway. So could you change it to Thursday? I'm at home all day Thursday. If that's a problem, give me a ring."]]},{ctx:"You hear a teacher talking to a class about a trip.",q:"What is she mainly doing?",o:["reminding them what to bring","explaining why the plan has changed","telling them how to behave"],a:1,x:"‘the coach company let us down, so we're going by train instead’ – the whole message explains the change. She mentions lunch and behaviour only in passing.",script:[["F","Before you go, a quick word about Friday. The coach company let us down, so we're going by train instead. That means meeting at the station at half past eight, not at school. Bring a packed lunch as before, and obviously behave yourselves on the train – but the main thing to remember is the new meeting place. Tell your parents tonight."]]},{ctx:"You hear a young man talking about his first week in a new job.",q:"How does he feel now?",o:["confident about the work itself","worried about the hours","disappointed by his colleagues"],a:0,x:"‘the actual work I can do – that bit I'm not worried about’. The hours are ‘fine’ and the team ‘couldn't have been friendlier’.",script:[["M","Honestly, the first two days I went home with a headache – so many names, so many systems. The team couldn't have been friendlier, though, and the hours are fine, better than my last place. By Thursday something clicked. The actual work I can do – that bit I'm not worried about. It's just learning where everything is."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a film she has seen.",q:"What does she praise about it?",o:["the acting","the music","the photography"],a:2,x:"‘every single shot looked like a painting’. She calls the acting ‘solid’ (not praise) and says the music was ‘far too loud’.",script:[["F","I wasn't expecting much, but every single shot looked like a painting – the light on the water, the empty streets. I could have watched it with the sound off, actually, because the music was far too loud in places. The acting was solid, nothing more, and the story is one you've seen before. But visually, it's the best thing I've seen this year."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about a holiday flat they have rented.",q:"What problem do they have with it?",o:["It is not as clean as they expected.","It is further from the beach than advertised.","It is noisier than they expected."],a:2,x:"‘the bar underneath doesn't close until two’. The flat was ‘spotless’ and the walk to the beach is ‘exactly as described’.",script:[["F","It's spotless, I'll give them that."],["M","And the beach really is five minutes, exactly as described."],["F","So why do you look so miserable?"],["M","Because the bar underneath doesn't close until two. I didn't sleep at all."],["F","Ah. I did wonder what that thumping was."]]},{ctx:"You hear a sports coach talking to a player.",q:"What is the coach doing?",o:["apologising for a decision","encouraging the player before a match","explaining why the player is not in the team"],a:2,x:"‘I'm leaving you out on Saturday’ and the reasons follow. He is positive about the future but he is not apologising.",script:[["M","Sit down a minute. I'm leaving you out on Saturday. Before you say anything – it's not about your attitude, which has been first class. You've played nine games in a row, and in the last two you've looked exhausted. I'd rather rest you now than lose you for a month. You'll be back for the cup game, and you'll be fresh."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Rosie Hart talking about keeping bees in a city.",script:[["F",`Good evening, everyone. My name's Rosie Hart, and I've been keeping bees here in the middle of the city for eleven years. People always ask how I started, and the honest answer is that I didn't choose it – my sister bought me a beekeeping course for my thirtieth birthday. I think she expected me to go once and forget about it. Instead I came home and ordered my first hive that same week.

Now, where do you put a hive when you live in a flat? My hives are on the roof of a library, which the council agreed to after about a year of polite letters. It's a wonderful spot: the bees fly off over the park and nobody below has any idea they're there.

The numbers surprise people. In the middle of summer, a single hive can hold up to fifty thousand bees. That's one box, one queen, and fifty thousand of her daughters. They'll travel up to five kilometres to find flowers, which means my bees visit gardens I've never even seen.

Everyone asks about equipment. You need a suit and gloves, of course, but the most important tool is a smoker. A little cool smoke at the entrance and the whole colony calms down – it's the difference between a pleasant afternoon and a very short one.

Beginners always worry about being stung. I won't pretend it doesn't happen, but after a while you barely notice. The thing nobody warns you about is the weight. A full box of honey can be twenty-five kilos, and you're lifting it above your head, on a roof, in a suit, in August.

City honey is better than most people expect. Mine tastes of lime, because the streets around the library are planted with lime trees, and for three weeks in July that's all the bees are interested in. Every neighbourhood tastes different.

In winter the bees don't hibernate, which is a common misunderstanding. They eat the honey they've stored and form a cluster, shivering their wing muscles to keep the queen at about thirty-five degrees. My job is simply to leave them enough food and not to keep opening the hive to have a look.

I sell what's left at a market on Saturdays. I never planned to sell anything, but eleven years of jars adds up, and it pays for the equipment.

If you're tempted, my advice is this: before you buy a single thing, join a local association. Someone experienced standing next to you at your first inspection is worth more than any book, and most associations will lend you a suit for the summer.`]],items:[{s:"Rosie was given a beekeeping {g} by her sister for her thirtieth birthday.",a:["course"],x:"‘my sister bought me a beekeeping course for my thirtieth birthday’"},{s:"Rosie keeps her hives on the roof of a {g}.",a:["library"],x:"‘My hives are on the roof of a library’"},{s:"In summer one hive can hold as many as {g} bees.",a:["fifty thousand","50,000","50000"],x:"‘a single hive can hold up to fifty thousand bees’"},{s:"Rosie's bees will fly up to {g} kilometres to find flowers.",a:["five","5"],x:"‘They'll travel up to five kilometres to find flowers’"},{s:"Rosie says the most important piece of equipment is a {g}.",a:["smoker"],x:"‘the most important tool is a smoker’ – the suit and gloves are mentioned first as distractors."},{s:"Rosie warns that beginners are not prepared for the {g} of a full box of honey.",a:["weight"],x:"‘The thing nobody warns you about is the weight.’"},{s:"Rosie's honey tastes of {g} because of the trees near the library.",a:["lime"],x:"‘Mine tastes of lime, because the streets around the library are planted with lime trees’"},{s:"In winter the bees eat their honey and form a {g} to keep the queen warm.",a:["cluster"],x:"‘They eat the honey they've stored and form a cluster’"},{s:"Rosie sells her honey at a {g} on Saturdays.",a:["market"],x:"‘I sell what's left at a market on Saturdays.’"},{s:"Rosie advises beginners to join a local {g} before buying anything.",a:["association"],x:"‘before you buy a single thing, join a local association’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about learning to cook.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about learning to cook. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I wanted to eat more healthily.","I needed to spend less money.","I was taught by an older relative.","I wanted to impress somebody.","I had to cook for somebody who was ill.","I started after a holiday abroad.","I wanted a hobby away from screens.","I learned because my job required it."],speakers:[{a:5,x:"F: two weeks in Italy and a cookery afternoon there – ‘I came home and started trying to make those things myself.’ Money is mentioned but as a result, not a reason (B).",script:[["F","Before that holiday I lived on toast, honestly. We spent two weeks in a small town in Italy, and the woman who owned the flat we rented spent an afternoon showing us how she made pasta – by hand, no machine. I came home and started trying to make those things myself. It's saved me a fortune since, but that wasn't why I started. It was that afternoon."]]},{a:6,x:"G: he stares at screens all day and wanted ‘something that used my hands and had nothing to do with a screen’. Health is a side effect (A).",script:[["M","I stare at three monitors for nine hours a day, and by the evening I'd be scrolling on my phone as well. I wanted something that used my hands and had nothing to do with a screen. Cooking turned out to be perfect – you can't check your email with flour all over your fingers. I eat better now too, which my doctor is pleased about, but that came later."]]},{a:4,x:"E: his mother came out of hospital and couldn't stand for long, so he cooked for her.",script:[["M","When Mum came out of hospital, she couldn't stand up for more than a few minutes, and someone had to feed the pair of us. My sister lives two hours away, so it was me. I started with soup, because it's hard to ruin, and worked up from there. By the time she was better I'd got quite good, and I've never stopped."]]},{a:1,x:"B: rent went up and takeaways were ‘the first thing to go’.",script:[["F","My rent went up by a hundred and fifty pounds last year, and something had to give. The takeaways were the first thing to go – I worked out I was spending forty pounds a week on food I hadn't even chosen properly. So I learned to cook about eight things really well, and I make them on repeat. It's not exciting, but my bank balance is much happier."]]},{a:3,x:"D: she invited someone she liked to dinner and ‘couldn't very well serve beans on toast’. Her grandmother is mentioned but as the source of a recipe, not the reason (C).",script:[["F","There was someone at work I rather liked, and in a moment of confidence I invited him round for dinner. Then I realised I couldn't very well serve beans on toast. I rang my grandmother in a panic and she talked me through her chicken recipe on the phone, twice. It went well – both the dinner and, for a while, the man. And I've cooked ever since."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a man called Dan Whitlock, who is a volunteer with a mountain rescue team.",script:[["F","My guest today is Dan Whitlock, who spends his weekends – and quite a few nights – rescuing people from the mountains. Dan, how did you get involved?"],["M","People assume something dramatic happened to me, but it didn't. I'd been walking in the hills for years quite safely. It was seeing the team at work one afternoon – they came past us carrying someone down, and they were so calm and so organised. I thought, I'd like to be able to do that. I applied the following week."],["F","What was the training like?"],["M","Long. Eighteen months before I was allowed on a call-out on my own. I expected the ropes and the first aid to be the hard part, and they were demanding, certainly. But what I hadn't imagined was how much navigation there'd be. You're finding a specific rock in the dark, in cloud, with a map and a compass, because you can't rely on a phone signal. That took me the longest to get right."],["F","And the equipment – that must cost a fortune."],["M","It does, and that's the part the public doesn't see. We're a charity; we get no government funding at all. A stretcher is three thousand pounds, a radio set more. People are very generous when we rattle a tin at the supermarket, but I do wish they knew the ambulance service doesn't pay for us."],["F","What do most of your call-outs involve?"],["M","Not what you'd think. Broken ankles, mostly – somebody slips on wet rock a mile from the car park. Real climbing accidents are rare. The single biggest cause of trouble, though, is simply running out of daylight. People set off at two in the afternoon in November and are surprised when it's dark at four."],["F","Does it annoy you, going out to people who weren't prepared?"],["M","I'm asked that a lot, and the honest answer is no. Anyone can misjudge a day out – I have. What does frustrate me is when people don't ring us because they're embarrassed, and then we're looking for them at midnight in the rain instead of at six o'clock in the light. Call early. Nobody will tell you off."],["F","How does your family feel about it?"],["M","My wife was worried at the start, and I understand why. What helped was when she came to an open day and saw the systems – how careful it all is. She's on the fundraising committee now. My daughter, who's nine, thinks it's completely normal that Dad leaves halfway through Sunday lunch."],["F","And what next?"],["M","I've started training as a search dog handler, which takes another two years. The dogs find people in minutes that twenty of us would take hours to find. If I'm honest, though, the real reason is that I've always wanted an excuse to spend that much time with a dog."]],qs:[{q:"Why did Dan join the mountain rescue team?",o:["He had been in danger in the mountains himself.","He was impressed when he watched the team working.","A member of the team encouraged him to apply."],a:1,x:"‘It was seeing the team at work one afternoon… I thought, I'd like to be able to do that.’ He says nothing dramatic happened to him."},{q:"What did Dan find most difficult during the training?",o:["the navigation","the rope work","the first aid"],a:0,x:"The ropes and first aid were ‘demanding’, but navigation ‘took me the longest to get right’."},{q:"What does Dan want the public to understand about the equipment?",o:["that donated money is spent carefully","that the team is not paid for by the state","that modern equipment is safer"],a:1,x:"‘We're a charity; we get no government funding at all… the ambulance service doesn't pay for us.’"},{q:"According to Dan, what causes most problems in the mountains?",o:["climbing accidents","sudden changes in the weather","people running out of daylight"],a:2,x:"‘The single biggest cause of trouble, though, is simply running out of daylight.’ Broken ankles are the most common injury, but daylight causes the trouble."},{q:"How does Dan feel about people who set off unprepared?",o:["He accepts that anyone can make a mistake.","He thinks they should pay for the rescue.","He believes they should be given advice at the car park."],a:0,x:"‘Anyone can misjudge a day out – I have.’ What frustrates him is people waiting too long to call."},{q:"What does Dan say about his family?",o:["His wife still finds it difficult.","His wife now helps the team.","His daughter worries when he leaves."],a:1,x:"‘She's on the fundraising committee now.’ His daughter thinks it is normal."},{q:"Why is Dan training as a dog handler?",o:["because dogs search far more quickly","because the team is short of handlers","mainly because he wants to work with a dog"],a:2,x:"He gives the speed as the official reason, then: ‘If I'm honest, though, the real reason is that I've always wanted an excuse to spend that much time with a dog.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l5",title:"Listening Test 5",topics:"Everyday situations · A cave guide · Keeping a diary · A sign language interpreter",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a jacket she bought online.",q:"What disappointed her about it?",o:["the colour","the size","the material"],a:2,x:"‘it's thin – the kind of cloth that's never going to keep out the rain’. The colour is ‘exactly like the photo’ and the size ‘fits perfectly’.",script:[["F","So the jacket arrived. The colour's exactly like the photo, which surprised me, because it never is. And it fits perfectly – I was worried, ordering a size I don't normally wear. But the moment I took it out of the bag I knew. It's thin – the kind of cloth that's never going to keep out the rain. For a winter jacket, that's not ideal. I'll probably send it back."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a podcast.",q:"What do they agree about it?",o:["The episodes are too long.","The presenters work well together.","The topics are carefully chosen."],a:1,x:"He says ‘the two of them bounce off each other’ and she agrees: ‘that's what keeps me listening’. She thinks the episodes are long but he doesn't mind; the topics are ‘a bit random’.",script:[["M","Have you listened to the new episode yet?"],["F","Halfway. They're getting longer, aren't they? Ninety minutes is a lot."],["M","I don't mind that, I listen on the train. What I like is that the two of them bounce off each other. Neither one tries to be the star."],["F","No, you're right, that's what keeps me listening. Because the topics are a bit random, let's be honest."],["M","They are. Last week was cheese, this week was volcanoes."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man phoning a dental surgery.",q:"Why is he calling?",o:["to cancel an appointment","to change the time of an appointment","to ask about the cost of a treatment"],a:1,x:"‘is there any chance of moving it to later in the day?’ He still wants the appointment, and he says he already knows the price.",script:[["M","Hi, it's Tom Barker. I've got an appointment with Dr Shah on Friday at nine. I definitely still need it – the tooth's been bothering me all week – but I've just been told I have to be at a training day in the morning. Is there any chance of moving it to later in the day? I know what the filling costs, the receptionist told me last time, so it's just the time really. Thanks."]]},{ctx:"You hear a student talking about studying in a group.",q:"How does she feel about it now?",o:["She finds it helps her concentrate.","She thinks it wastes too much time.","She prefers it to working with a teacher."],a:0,x:"‘knowing the others are working stops me picking up my phone’ – it keeps her focused. She says the chatting ‘used to’ waste time, and never compares it with a teacher.",script:[["F","At first I thought study groups were just an excuse to chat, and to be fair, for the first couple of weeks that's mostly what we did. But we made a rule – first hour, nobody talks. And it's strange, but knowing the others are working stops me picking up my phone. On my own I'm checking messages every five minutes. Now I get more done in two hours there than in a whole evening at home."]]},{ctx:"You hear a radio presenter introducing a programme.",q:"What is the programme about?",o:["how bridges are designed","the history of one particular bridge","people who live near famous bridges"],a:0,x:"‘what goes on in an engineer's head when they're asked to cross a river’ – the design process. Old bridges and local people are mentioned only as examples of what the programme is not about.",script:[["M","Good evening. Tonight's programme isn't a history lesson – you won't hear about who opened which bridge in eighteen-something, and we haven't gone out interviewing the people who live underneath them, either. Instead, we're asking what goes on in an engineer's head when they're asked to cross a river. Why that shape? Why steel and not concrete? Our guest has spent thirty years answering those questions."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about volunteering at a library.",q:"Why did she start volunteering there?",o:["She wanted to meet new people.","She wanted experience for a future job.","She wanted to help the library stay open."],a:2,x:"‘the council said it might have to close… so a few of us offered to run the Saturday opening’. Meeting people was a result, and she says she has no plans to work in a library.",script:[["F","Three years ago the council said the library might have to close – there just wasn't the money for staff at weekends. I'd taken my children there every week since they were tiny, so a few of us offered to run the Saturday opening ourselves. I've made some lovely friends through it, which I didn't expect. People ask if I'm thinking of training as a librarian, but no – I just didn't want to lose the place."]]},{ctx:"You hear two neighbours talking about a new cycle lane.",q:"What is the man's opinion of it?",o:["It has made the street more dangerous.","It was a good idea but badly planned.","It is unnecessary because few people cycle."],a:1,x:"‘I'm all for it in principle… but whoever drew it… stops dead at the roundabout’. He supports the idea; she is the one who mentions how few cyclists there are, and he disagrees.",script:[["F","I've hardly seen anyone use it, to be honest."],["M","Give it time – it's only been open a month, and more people cycle round here than you'd think. I'm all for it in principle. But whoever drew it can't have actually ridden it. It stops dead at the roundabout, so you're thrown straight back into the traffic at the worst possible point."],["F","So it's not safer at all, then?"],["M","No, it is safer on the straight bits. It's just the ending."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a second-hand bookshop.",q:"What does he like most about it?",o:["the owner's recommendations","the low prices","the café upstairs"],a:0,x:"‘the best thing is the owner – you tell her what you've enjoyed and she finds you something you'd never have picked up’. The books are ‘not especially cheap’ and the café is ‘nothing special’.",script:[["M","The books aren't especially cheap, if I'm honest – you can find most of them for less online. And there's a little café upstairs, which is nice enough, but nothing special. The best thing is the owner. You tell her what you've enjoyed lately, and she goes off into the back and comes out with something you'd never have picked up yourself. She's hardly ever wrong. I go every couple of weeks now."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a man called Owen Davies talking about his work as a cave guide.",script:[["M",`Hello, everyone. I'm Owen Davies, and for the last nine years I've been taking visitors underground – I'm a guide in a system of limestone caves in the hills of South Wales. Before this I was an electrician, believe it or not. I came on a tour as a visitor one wet Sunday, got talking to the guide afterwards, and three months later I had his job, because he was retiring.

People imagine the caves are freezing. In fact the temperature down there stays at around eleven degrees all year, summer and winter. In August, visitors arrive in shorts and leave shivering; in January, the caves feel almost warm.

The tour most people book is the short one, about an hour, along lit paths. But what I enjoy most is the adventure tour, where groups crawl through narrow passages wearing helmets and lamps. The narrowest part is called the Letterbox, and it's exactly as unpleasant as it sounds.

The biggest chamber is enormous – you could fit a small church inside it. The thing that impresses visitors most, though, isn't the size. It's the moment I ask everyone to switch off their lamps. Most people have never experienced complete darkness before, and it's a strange, almost heavy feeling.

The rock formations grow incredibly slowly. A stalactite might grow just one centimetre in a hundred years, which is why touching them is strictly forbidden – the oil from one hand can stop a formation growing for good.

The caves aren't empty, either. We share them with a colony of bats, and from November to March part of the system is closed so we don't disturb them while they hibernate.

The worst part of the job isn't the darkness or the cold – it's the water. After heavy rain, the stream at the bottom can rise very quickly, and we cancel tours if the level passes a marker painted on the wall.

The question I'm asked most often is whether anyone's ever been lost down there. Not on my tours! But every guide carries a radio, and we count everyone in and out.

If you'd like to try caving yourself, my advice is simple: go with a club first. They'll lend you equipment, and you'll learn far more from experienced cavers than from any video.`]],items:[{s:"Before becoming a guide, Owen worked as an {g}.",a:["electrician","an electrician"],x:"‘Before this I was an electrician’"},{s:"Owen got his job when the previous guide was {g}.",a:["retiring"],x:"‘three months later I had his job, because he was retiring’"},{s:"The temperature in the caves stays at about {g} degrees all year.",a:["eleven","11"],x:"‘the temperature down there stays at around eleven degrees all year’"},{s:"On the adventure tour, visitors wear helmets and {g}.",a:["lamps"],x:"‘crawl through narrow passages wearing helmets and lamps’"},{s:"The narrowest part of the adventure tour is known as the {g}.",a:["Letterbox","letter box","letterbox"],x:"‘The narrowest part is called the Letterbox’"},{s:"Owen says the biggest chamber could hold a small {g}.",a:["church"],x:"‘you could fit a small church inside it’"},{s:"A stalactite may grow only one {g} in a hundred years.",a:["centimetre","centimeter","cm"],x:"‘A stalactite might grow just one centimetre in a hundred years’"},{s:"Part of the cave system is closed in winter because of the {g} living there.",a:["bats","colony of bats"],x:"‘We share them with a colony of bats… so we don't disturb them while they hibernate’"},{s:"Owen says the worst part of the job is the {g}.",a:["water"],x:"‘The worst part of the job isn't the darkness or the cold – it's the water.’"},{s:"Owen advises beginners to go caving with a {g} first.",a:["club"],x:"‘go with a club first’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about keeping a diary.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about keeping a diary. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I was given a diary as a present.","I only write when something important happens.","I write on my phone rather than on paper.","Reading old entries can be embarrassing.","A doctor suggested it.","It helps me sleep.","I started because of a long journey.","I've stopped writing it."],speakers:[{a:6,x:"G: ‘I bought a notebook at the airport before six months in South America… and I've filled one every year since’. He writes on paper, so C is wrong.",script:[["M","I bought a cheap notebook at the airport before six months travelling in South America, because I was sure I'd forget everything otherwise. I wrote every single night, in hostels, on buses. When I got home I just carried on, and I've filled one every year since. Always paper – I tried an app once and it felt like work."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘I read some of my fourteen-year-old self's entries… I wanted to hide under the table’. She still writes every day (H is wrong).",script:[["F","I've kept one since I was twelve, more or less every day. Last Christmas my sister found a box of the old ones in our parents' loft and made me read some out. My fourteen-year-old self was so dramatic – every boy was the love of my life. I wanted to hide under the table. But I still write every night before bed."]]},{a:1,x:"B: ‘weeks go by without a word… then something big happens and I'll write five pages’. The diary was bought by himself, not a present (A).",script:[["M","I'm not one of those people who write every day. Honestly, weeks go by without a word. Then something big happens – a new job, a break-up, my daughter being born – and I'll sit down and write five pages. I bought the notebook myself years ago; it's still only half full. But it's a record of all the moments that mattered."]]},{a:5,x:"F: ‘if I write it all down, I'm asleep in ten minutes’. It was her own idea after reading an article, not a doctor's (E).",script:[["F","I used to lie awake going over everything – what I'd said in a meeting, what I'd forgotten to do. I read an article about writing things down before bed, and I thought, why not? Now, if I write it all down, I'm asleep in ten minutes. It's not a beautiful diary – mostly lists – but it works better than anything else I've tried."]]},{a:7,x:"H: ‘I haven't written a word for about two years’. He bought the diary himself, so A is wrong.",script:[["M","I treated myself to a lovely leather diary when I started university, and I filled it for about three years. Then I got busy, and honestly, I haven't written a word for about two years now. I still have it on my shelf. Every so often I think I should start again, but somehow I never do."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a woman called Grace Adeyemi, who is a sign language interpreter at concerts and festivals.",script:[["M","My guest today is Grace Adeyemi, who interprets live music into British Sign Language for deaf audiences. Grace, how did you start?"],["F","I grew up with a deaf father, so signing was always part of family life – but I never thought of it as a job. I trained as a teacher. Then a friend who worked at a festival rang me in a panic because their interpreter had cancelled, and I said yes before I'd really thought about it. That was eight years ago."],["M","Interpreting a song must be very different from interpreting a speech."],["F","Completely. With a speech, you're carrying the meaning of the words. With music, the words are only part of it. I have to show the rhythm, whether the drums are heavy, when the guitar takes over. A lot of deaf people feel the bass through the floor, so what they want from me is everything they can't feel – the mood, and the lyrics."],["M","How do you prepare?"],["F","The bands send me their set list, if I'm lucky, about a week before. I listen to every song dozens of times. The lyrics are usually online, but they're often wrong, so I can't rely on them. And I watch videos of the band live, because singers change things on stage – they repeat a chorus, or talk to the crowd between songs."],["M","Is there a type of music you find particularly difficult?"],["F","People expect me to say fast rap, and that is hard, but at least the words are clear. The real challenge is songs that are deliberately unclear – where even hearing people argue about what the lyrics mean. I have to choose a meaning, and I know some of the audience will disagree with my choice."],["M","Do the musicians ever get involved?"],["F","More and more. Some bands now ask me to stand on the main stage rather than at the side, which makes a huge difference – deaf fans don't have to choose between watching me and watching the band. One singer even learnt to sign the chorus of her biggest hit. The crowd went wild."],["M","What's the most rewarding part of the job?"],["F","Before a gig, a young deaf woman once told me it was the first concert she'd been to without feeling like an outsider. That stays with me. The applause is nice, and so is the travel, but that's why I do it."],["M","Finally – any advice for someone thinking of becoming an interpreter?"],["F","Spend time in the deaf community before you do any course. Classes will give you the grammar, but the community gives you the language as it's really used – the jokes, the slang. You can't get that from a textbook."]],qs:[{q:"How did Grace begin working as an interpreter at music events?",o:["She agreed to help when another interpreter was unavailable.","She trained for it after leaving her teaching job.","Her father encouraged her to use her signing skills."],a:0,x:"‘their interpreter had cancelled, and I said yes before I'd really thought about it’. She never thought of signing as a job, and her father isn't said to have encouraged her."},{q:"What does Grace say deaf audiences mainly want from her?",o:["a translation of what the singer says between songs","information they cannot get from the vibrations","a clear signal of when each song starts"],a:1,x:"‘A lot of deaf people feel the bass through the floor, so what they want from me is everything they can't feel – the mood, and the lyrics.’"},{q:"Why does Grace watch videos of bands performing live?",o:["because printed lyrics are hard to find","because she wants to learn the band's style of dancing","because songs are often performed differently on stage"],a:2,x:"‘singers change things on stage – they repeat a chorus, or talk to the crowd’. The lyrics are online but often wrong – that's a different point."},{q:"Which songs does Grace find most difficult to interpret?",o:["songs where the meaning of the words is uncertain","songs that are performed very quickly","songs with a lot of instrumental parts"],a:0,x:"‘The real challenge is songs that are deliberately unclear… I have to choose a meaning.’ Fast rap is hard ‘but at least the words are clear’."},{q:"Why does Grace prefer to stand on the main stage?",o:["Musicians can follow her signs more easily.","Deaf fans can see her and the band at the same time.","The sound is clearer for her there."],a:1,x:"‘deaf fans don't have to choose between watching me and watching the band’"},{q:"What does Grace find most rewarding about her job?",o:["the reaction from the crowd","the chance to travel","making deaf people feel included"],a:2,x:"A young woman said it was ‘the first concert she'd been to without feeling like an outsider… that's why I do it’. Applause and travel are ‘nice’ but not the reason."},{q:"What advice does Grace give to future interpreters?",o:["to take a course as early as possible","to get to know deaf people before studying","to practise by interpreting songs at home"],a:1,x:"‘Spend time in the deaf community before you do any course.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l6",title:"Listening Test 6",topics:"Everyday situations · A tree surgeon · Team sports in adult life · A young solo sailor",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a hotel he stayed in.",q:"What was he unhappy about?",o:["the noise at night","the breakfast","the internet connection"],a:2,x:"‘I couldn't get online in the room at all, and I'd gone there to work’. The street was ‘surprisingly quiet’ and breakfast was ‘more than I could eat’.",script:[["M","It's right in the centre, but the street was surprisingly quiet at night, which I hadn't expected. Breakfast was more than I could eat – they kept bringing things. The problem was I couldn't get online in the room at all, and I'd gone there to work. I ended up sitting in the lobby with my laptop every evening. For a business hotel, that's a strange thing to get wrong."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about concert tickets.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The tickets were too expensive.","It was difficult to buy the tickets.","Their seats are in a good position."],a:1,x:"She says ‘forty minutes in a queue online’ and he agrees ‘the website kept crashing for me too’. He thinks the price was fair, and she isn't sure about the seats.",script:[["F","I finally got them, but it took me forty minutes in a queue online."],["M","Same here – the website kept crashing for me too. I nearly gave up."],["F","And then sixty pounds each. That's a lot for two hours."],["M","For that band? I'd have paid more, honestly."],["F","Well, let's hope we can actually see the stage. Row Z didn't sound promising."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman leaving a message for a plumber.",q:"Why is she calling?",o:["to explain where he can park","to say the job is now more urgent","to ask him to come at a different time"],a:0,x:"‘the road outside is closed tomorrow, so you'll need to park behind the shop on Mill Lane’. The time stays the same and the leak ‘isn't any worse’.",script:[["F","Hi, it's Mrs Okafor at 14 Station Road. You're coming at ten tomorrow to look at the leak under the sink – that's still fine, and it isn't any worse, so don't worry. It's just that the road outside is closed tomorrow for resurfacing, so you'll need to park behind the shop on Mill Lane and walk round. It's only a minute. See you at ten."]]},{ctx:"You hear a teenager talking about his part-time job in a café.",q:"What does he enjoy most about the job?",o:["earning his own money","chatting to the customers","learning to make coffee"],a:1,x:"‘the regulars are what makes it – I know half of them by name now and we have a laugh’. The money is ‘not much’ and making coffee is ‘harder than it looks’, not his favourite part.",script:[["M","The money's not much, if I'm honest – it pays for my phone and that's about it. And making the coffee is harder than it looks; I still can't do the patterns on the top. But the regulars are what makes it. I know half of them by name now, and we have a laugh. There's an old man who comes in every Saturday and tells me what I'm doing wrong. I'd miss him."]]},{ctx:"You hear a guide speaking to visitors at a museum.",q:"What is she doing?",o:["describing the most popular part of the museum","explaining a change to the visit","giving advice about taking photos"],a:1,x:"‘the upper gallery is closed this morning… so we'll start downstairs instead and go up after lunch’. Photos and the popular dinosaur room are mentioned but they aren't the purpose.",script:[["F","Good morning, everyone. Just before we start – I'm afraid the upper gallery is closed this morning while they repair the lighting. So rather than following the plan on your leaflet, we'll start downstairs with the dinosaurs, which is everyone's favourite anyway, and go up after lunch when it's open again. Photos are fine everywhere, as long as you switch off the flash."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a language-learning app.",q:"Why does she like it?",o:["It is free to use.","The lessons are very short.","It corrects her pronunciation."],a:1,x:"‘each lesson is three minutes, so I can do one while the kettle boils’. She pays for it, and she says the pronunciation feature ‘doesn't really work’.",script:[["F","I pay for it, which I resisted for ages, but I've stuck with it longer than any course I've ever done. The reason is simple: each lesson is three minutes, so I can do one while the kettle boils, or waiting for the bus. There's supposedly a feature that listens to your pronunciation, but it doesn't really work – it says everything's perfect. Still, I'm on day two hundred."]]},{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about a training course.",q:"What is the man's opinion of it?",o:["It was a waste of time.","It was too difficult for beginners.","It was useful but too long."],a:2,x:"‘I did learn things I'll actually use… but three full days? It could have been one.’ He disagrees that it was a waste, and says the level was ‘fine’.",script:[["F","So how was the course? Three days away from your desk."],["M","Honestly? I did learn things I'll actually use – the spreadsheet stuff especially. But three full days? It could easily have been one."],["F","Jake said it was a complete waste of time."],["M","That's not fair. And the level was fine – they started from the basics."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a camping trip.",q:"What went wrong?",o:["They forgot some equipment.","The campsite was full when they arrived.","The weather was worse than forecast."],a:0,x:"‘we'd left the tent poles in the garage’. They had booked the campsite, and the rain was ‘exactly what they'd said on the forecast’.",script:[["M","We'd booked a pitch weeks ago, so at least we knew we had somewhere to go. It rained, but that was exactly what they'd said on the forecast, so we'd packed for it. Then we unrolled the tent and realised we'd left the tent poles in the garage, two hundred kilometres away. We spent the first night in the car. The kids thought it was the best holiday ever."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Hannah Cole talking about her work as a tree surgeon.",script:[["F",`Hi, everyone. I'm Hannah Cole, and I'm a tree surgeon, which means I look after trees – cutting, repairing, and sometimes, sadly, taking them down. People often think the job is just about chainsaws, but I spend almost as much time with a notebook as with a saw.

I came to it quite late. I studied geography at university and then worked in an office for six years, and the thing I hated most was never being outside. I did a short climbing course for fun, the instructor turned out to be a tree surgeon, and that was that.

The climbing is the part people ask about. I'm attached to the tree by two ropes at all times, never one. If you only remember one thing from today, remember that.

The tallest tree I've worked in was a cedar, thirty-four metres high, in the grounds of an old school. From the top you could see the sea, which is about twenty kilometres away.

Most of my work isn't in forests, by the way – it's in people's gardens and in city streets. And the most common reason I'm called out isn't storms, as people expect. It's that a tree has grown too close to a house, and its roots are causing problems with the walls.

Before I cut anything, I check the tree for wildlife. In spring, nesting birds are protected by law, so if I find a nest, the work simply waits until autumn. Customers aren't always happy about that.

The hardest part of the job isn't physical, it's telling people their favourite tree has to go. I once had to tell an elderly man that the oak his father planted was dying. He made me a cup of tea and told me stories about it for an hour.

Nothing goes to waste afterwards. Larger trunks go to a local furniture maker, and the smaller branches are turned into chips that we give away for garden paths.

If you're interested in the job, my advice is to start with a tree identification course. Knowing exactly what species you're looking at is the foundation of everything else.`]],items:[{s:"Hannah says she spends a lot of time using a {g} as well as a saw.",a:["notebook"],x:"‘I spend almost as much time with a notebook as with a saw’"},{s:"At university, Hannah studied {g}.",a:["geography"],x:"‘I studied geography at university’"},{s:"Hannah met a tree surgeon through a {g} course she did for fun.",a:["climbing","short climbing"],x:"‘I did a short climbing course for fun, the instructor turned out to be a tree surgeon’"},{s:"When climbing, Hannah is always attached by {g} ropes.",a:["two","2"],x:"‘I'm attached to the tree by two ropes at all times, never one’"},{s:"The tallest tree Hannah has worked in was a {g}.",a:["cedar"],x:"‘a cedar, thirty-four metres high’"},{s:"Hannah is most often called out because a tree is too close to a {g}.",a:["house"],x:"‘a tree has grown too close to a house’. Storms are the distractor."},{s:"If Hannah finds a {g}, she has to postpone the work until autumn.",a:["nest"],x:"‘if I find a nest, the work simply waits until autumn’"},{s:"An elderly man's oak tree had been planted by his {g}.",a:["father"],x:"‘the oak his father planted was dying’"},{s:"Large tree trunks are given to a local {g}.",a:["furniture maker"],x:"‘Larger trunks go to a local furniture maker’"},{s:"Hannah recommends starting with a course in tree {g}.",a:["identification"],x:"‘start with a tree identification course’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about joining a sports team as an adult.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about joining the team. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I was worried about not being good enough.","I joined because I'd moved to a new area.","I enjoy the social side more than the sport.","I got injured soon after starting.","A doctor advised me to take more exercise.","I had played the sport as a child.","I found the training harder than the matches.","I now help to run the team."],speakers:[{a:1,x:"B: ‘I didn't know a single person in the town… a netball team seemed the quickest way to meet people.’ She'd never played before (F).",script:[["F","When I moved here for work, I didn't know a single person in the town. Evenings were very long. A netball team seemed the quickest way to meet people, even though I'd never played in my life – we did hockey at my school. It worked, too. Within a month I had more friends here than I had in my old city."]]},{a:7,x:"H: ‘now I organise the fixtures and I'm the treasurer as well’. He mentions the pub afterwards but says he's there for the football (C).",script:[["M","I joined as a player three years ago, and for a while that's all I was. Then the man who did everything left, and somebody had to. So now I organise the fixtures and I'm the treasurer as well, which means chasing people for their subscriptions. Some people come for the pub afterwards, but I'm still there for the football."]]},{a:0,x:"A: ‘I nearly didn't go… I was sure everyone would be far better than me.’ Training and matches are mentioned only to say it was fine.",script:[["F","I nearly didn't go to the first session. I stood outside the sports hall for ten minutes, because I was sure everyone would be far better than me and I'd let the side down. In fact, half of them had only started that year. The training's fine, the matches are fine – it was all in my head."]]},{a:4,x:"E: ‘she said, quite firmly, that I needed to do something more active’. He had never played basketball before (F).",script:[["M","I'd never played basketball in my life – at school I was always the one picked last for any team. What actually got me into it was my doctor. My blood pressure was creeping up, and she said, quite firmly, that I needed to do something more active than walking to the car. So I found a veterans' team. I'm the slowest on the court, but I've lost six kilos."]]},{a:6,x:"G: ‘the matches are the easy part… it's the Tuesday training that nearly kills me’. She hasn't been injured (D) – she says ‘touch wood, nothing so far’.",script:[["F","People assume the matches must be the tough bit, but actually the matches are the easy part – you're so caught up in the game you don't notice how tired you are. It's the Tuesday training that nearly kills me. Ninety minutes of running and drills in the cold. Injuries? Touch wood, nothing so far, but I ache for two days afterwards."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a woman called Ellie Marsh, who has sailed around Britain on her own.",script:[["M","With me is Ellie Marsh, who at nineteen sailed alone all the way round Britain. Ellie, where did the idea come from?"],["F","Not from a big dream, I'm afraid. I'd been sailing since I was eight, and in my last year at school a friend's dad lent me a book about a woman who'd done the trip in the nineteen-sixties. I read it in one night. What got me wasn't the adventure – it was that she'd done it in a boat smaller than mine. I thought, well, then I've got no excuse."],["M","How long did you spend planning?"],["F","Nearly two years, and most of it wasn't on the water. I had to learn to fix everything myself – the engine, the electrics, the sails – because you can't call anyone when you're thirty miles out. The sailing I already knew. The repairs were the real course."],["M","What was the hardest moment?"],["F","Everyone expects me to say a storm, and there were storms, but you're so busy in a storm you don't have time to be scared. The worst was off the north coast of Scotland, when there was no wind at all for three days. I just sat there, going nowhere, and that's when the loneliness really hit."],["M","Did you ever think about giving up?"],["F","Once. I'd hurt my hand and I couldn't pull the ropes properly. I actually rang my mum to say I was coming home. She didn't try to change my mind – she just asked whether I could wait two days and see. By then the swelling had gone down, and I carried on."],["M","And now you're back – what's changed?"],["F","I'm much more patient. I used to get annoyed if a bus was five minutes late. When you've waited three days for wind, a late bus is nothing. And I'm far better at being on my own – I actually enjoy it now."],["M","What's next?"],["F","People keep asking if I'll sail round the world. Maybe one day. Right now I'm starting a degree in marine engineering in September – partly because of all those repairs. I found out I loved fixing things almost more than sailing."]],qs:[{q:"What does Ellie say about the book she read?",o:["She found it difficult to finish.","She read it very quickly.","She read it several times before the trip."],a:1,x:"‘I read it in one night.’"},{q:"What made Ellie decide to sail around Britain?",o:["She wanted to have a great adventure.","She realised someone had done it in a smaller boat.","A friend's father offered to lend her a boat."],a:1,x:"‘What got me wasn't the adventure – it was that she'd done it in a boat smaller than mine.’ The friend's dad lent her a book, not a boat."},{q:"What did Ellie spend most of her preparation time learning?",o:["how to repair her equipment","how to sail in difficult conditions","how to plan a route"],a:0,x:"‘I had to learn to fix everything myself… The repairs were the real course.’ She already knew the sailing."},{q:"What was the worst moment of the trip for Ellie?",o:["being caught in a storm","a period with no wind","losing contact with land"],a:1,x:"‘The worst was… when there was no wind at all for three days… that's when the loneliness really hit.’ Storms kept her too busy to be scared."},{q:"How did Ellie's mother react when Ellie wanted to give up?",o:["She told Ellie to come home.","She persuaded Ellie that she could continue.","She suggested Ellie wait before deciding."],a:2,x:"‘She didn't try to change my mind – she just asked whether I could wait two days and see.’"},{q:"How has the trip changed Ellie?",o:["She feels more confident in a crisis.","She has become more patient.","She has become more sociable."],a:1,x:"‘I'm much more patient… a late bus is nothing.’ She enjoys being alone more, which is the opposite of more sociable."},{q:"Why is Ellie going to study marine engineering?",o:["She discovered she enjoys repairing things.","She needs it to sail around the world.","Her family thinks she needs a career."],a:0,x:"‘partly because of all those repairs. I found out I loved fixing things almost more than sailing.’ The round-the-world trip is only ‘maybe one day’."}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l7",title:"Listening Test 7",topics:"Everyday situations · A hot-air balloon pilot · Life without a car · A children's book illustrator",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a gym she has joined.",q:"What has surprised her about it?",o:["how friendly the other members are","how busy it is in the mornings","how much the classes cost"],a:0,x:"‘What I didn't expect was people actually saying hello… someone even lent me a towel’. She expected it to be busy and knew the classes were extra.",script:[["F","I knew it would be packed before work, everyone said so, and it is. And I knew the classes were extra – that was in the contract. What I didn't expect was people actually saying hello. At my old gym nobody took their headphones out for three years. Here, someone even lent me a towel on the first day when I forgot mine."]]},{ctx:"You hear two students talking about their exam timetable.",q:"What are they worried about?",o:["having two exams on the same day","not knowing which room to go to","having too little time between exams"],a:2,x:"‘history on Monday afternoon and then biology first thing Tuesday… no time to revise’ and he agrees ‘that's what's worrying me too’. Two exams in a day is ruled out; the room is ‘on the board’.",script:[["F","At least they haven't put two on the same day this time."],["M","No, but look – history on Monday afternoon and then biology first thing Tuesday. There's basically no time to revise in between."],["F","I know, that's what's worrying me too. One evening for all of biology."],["M","Do you know which room it's in?"],["F","It's on the board outside the office. That's the least of our problems."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a message for a friend.",q:"Why is he calling?",o:["to cancel their weekend plans","to suggest a different activity","to ask his friend for a lift"],a:1,x:"‘instead of the beach, how about the climbing wall?’ He still wants to meet, and he says he'll drive.",script:[["M","Hi Sam, it's Josh. Still on for Saturday? I've just seen the forecast – rain all day on the coast. So I was thinking, instead of the beach, how about the climbing wall in town? It's indoors, and they've got that new section. I'll drive, I can pick you up at ten. Let me know what you think."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a book club.",q:"Why does she enjoy it?",o:["It makes her read books she wouldn't choose.","It is a chance to meet new people.","The discussions are very serious."],a:0,x:"‘I'd never have picked up half of them myself… that's the point for me’. The members are old friends, and the discussions ‘end up being about our lives’, not serious.",script:[["F","There are only six of us, and we've known each other for years, so it's not about meeting anyone. And the discussions are hardly academic – they usually end up being about our lives. But we take turns choosing, and I'd never have picked up half of them myself. Science fiction, a book about whales – that's the point for me. It stops me reading the same kind of thing forever."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man speaking to passengers on a boat.",q:"What is he doing?",o:["describing the places they will see","warning them about the conditions","explaining how long the trip will take"],a:1,x:"‘once we're past the harbour wall it'll get quite rough… please stay seated and hold on to the rail’. The trip time and the seals are mentioned briefly.",script:[["M","Good afternoon and welcome aboard. Just so you know, once we're past the harbour wall it'll get quite rough for about ten minutes – the wind's picked up since this morning. So please stay seated and hold on to the rail, and keep children away from the sides. After that it calms down. We should still be back within the hour, and with luck we'll see the seals."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a dance class he has started.",q:"How does he feel about it now?",o:["embarrassed by his mistakes","proud of how much he has improved","keen to try a more difficult class"],a:1,x:"‘Last week I got through the whole routine without stopping. I was quite pleased with myself.’ The embarrassment was ‘at first’, and he isn't ready for the advanced class.",script:[["M","At first I was so embarrassed – I'd go left when everyone went right, and there are mirrors on every wall, so you can't hide. But I kept going, and last week I got through the whole routine without stopping. I was quite pleased with myself, actually. The teacher said I could try the advanced class in the spring. I'm not sure I'm that brave yet."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a TV series.",q:"What does the woman think of it?",o:["The ending was disappointing.","The middle episodes were too slow.","The main character was unconvincing."],a:0,x:"‘eight brilliant episodes and then the last one just… fell apart’. He thought the middle was slow, not her; she says the actor was ‘great’.",script:[["M","Did you finish it?"],["F","Last night. Eight brilliant episodes and then the last one just… fell apart. All those questions and no answers."],["M","I didn't even get that far. The middle dragged for me."],["F","Really? I loved the middle. And he was great as the detective, wasn't he? It's only the ending I can't forgive."]]},{ctx:"You hear a shop assistant talking to a customer.",q:"What is she doing?",o:["apologising for a mistake","offering an alternative product","explaining the shop's returns policy"],a:1,x:"‘we've got this one, which is very similar… and it's actually a bit cheaper’. She isn't apologising – the model is simply sold out – and returns aren't mentioned.",script:[["F","I'm afraid that model's completely sold out, and the supplier can't say when we'll get more. But we've got this one, which is very similar – same size, same speed, it just doesn't come in red. And it's actually a bit cheaper. Would you like me to get one down so you can have a look?"]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Priya Nair talking about her work as a hot-air balloon pilot.",script:[["F",`Good afternoon. I'm Priya Nair, and I fly hot-air balloons – mostly taking paying passengers over the countryside, and sometimes at festivals. I've been flying for fourteen years, and I'm still learning something new every season.

My route into ballooning was a strange one. I trained as a dentist's assistant, and a patient of ours was a balloon pilot. He offered me a free flight to say thank you for looking after his nerves, and I never really looked back.

The question everyone asks is how you steer a balloon. The honest answer is that you can't – not directly. You can only go up or down, and the wind blows in different directions at different heights. So steering means finding the right height for the direction you want.

We almost always fly at dawn or in the early evening. In the middle of the day, the sun heats the ground unevenly and the air becomes bumpy and unpredictable – which is fine for birds, but not for a basket full of passengers.

People are often surprised by the size of it all. The basket I use carries up to twelve passengers, and the balloon itself, fully inflated, is about as tall as a seven-storey building.

The most important thing I do before every flight isn't checking the burners, although of course I check them. It's the weather. I study the forecast the night before and again at four in the morning, and if the wind is wrong, we don't go. I cancel about a third of my flights.

The best part of the job is the silence. Between bursts of the burner there's no engine, no wind in your face – because you're moving with it – just birds and the sound of dogs barking on the farms below.

Landing is the part passengers are nervous about. We teach everyone a landing position before take-off: knees bent, back against the basket, holding the rope handles. Most landings are gentle, but now and then the basket tips onto its side and slides for a few metres.

Afterwards we celebrate in the traditional way, with a glass of something and a certificate for each passenger.

If you'd like to learn to fly yourself, my advice is to join a balloon crew as a volunteer first. You'll learn more about the balloon from packing it away in a wet field than you ever will in the air.`]],items:[{s:"Priya originally trained as a {g}.",a:["dentist's assistant","dentist assistant","dental assistant"],x:"‘I trained as a dentist's assistant’"},{s:"Priya's first flight was a gift from a {g}.",a:["patient","balloon pilot"],x:"‘a patient of ours was a balloon pilot. He offered me a free flight’"},{s:"To steer a balloon, the pilot has to find the right {g}.",a:["height"],x:"‘steering means finding the right height for the direction you want’"},{s:"Flights don't take place in the middle of the day because the air becomes {g}.",a:["bumpy","bumpy and unpredictable"],x:"‘the air becomes bumpy and unpredictable’"},{s:"Priya's basket can carry up to {g} passengers.",a:["twelve","12"],x:"‘The basket I use carries up to twelve passengers’"},{s:"A fully inflated balloon is as tall as a {g} building.",a:["seven-storey","seven storey","7-storey","7 storey"],x:"‘about as tall as a seven-storey building’"},{s:"Priya says the most important thing she checks before a flight is the {g}.",a:["weather"],x:"‘The most important thing I do before every flight isn't checking the burners… It's the weather.’"},{s:"Priya cancels about a {g} of her flights.",a:["third"],x:"‘I cancel about a third of my flights.’"},{s:"Priya says the best part of the job is the {g}.",a:["silence"],x:"‘The best part of the job is the silence.’"},{s:"Priya suggests that future pilots first join a balloon {g} as volunteers.",a:["crew"],x:"‘join a balloon crew as a volunteer first’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about living without a car.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about living without a car. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I had no choice at first.","I've become much fitter.","It has made shopping more difficult.","I spend more time with my neighbours.","I sometimes borrow a car.","I've saved more money than I expected.","My children were against the idea.","I plan to buy a car again soon."],speakers:[{a:0,x:"A: ‘I failed my eyesight test… so the decision was made for me.’ He now cycles but says he isn't ‘any fitter, sadly’ (B).",script:[["M","I'd love to say it was a noble decision, but I failed my eyesight test when I turned seventy, so the decision was made for me. I was furious for about a month. Now I've got a bus pass and a tricycle with a basket, and I quite like it. I'm not any fitter, sadly – the tricycle's electric."]]},{a:5,x:"F: ‘I'd worked out I'd save about a thousand a year… it's turned out to be nearer three.’ Shopping is ‘no problem’ because it's delivered (C).",script:[["F","When we sold the car, I'd worked out I'd save about a thousand a year, after paying for the odd taxi. It's turned out to be nearer three, because I'd forgotten about parking, repairs, all the little things. Shopping's no problem – it's delivered on Thursdays. I put the difference into a holiday fund."]]},{a:4,x:"E: ‘my brother lends me his for the big supermarket trip once a month’. He has no plans to buy one (H).",script:[["M","I won't pretend I never use one. My brother lends me his for the big supermarket trip once a month, and when we visit my parents in the countryside. The rest of the time it's the bike and the train. Buy one again? Not unless we move out of the city. It would just sit in the street."]]},{a:6,x:"G: ‘the kids were horrified… they thought it meant no more trips to football’. Fitness is mentioned only as a joke about the kids.",script:[["F","Honestly, the kids were horrified. They were ten and twelve, and they thought it meant no more trips to football, no more lifts to friends' houses. There were a lot of arguments. Two years on, they cycle everywhere and they're the fittest children in the street. But they still remind me how unfair I was."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘walking to the station every day, I've got to know half the street… we have each other's keys now’. He chose it, so A is wrong.",script:[["M","It was my choice – I just got tired of paying for something that sat outside the house. The thing I didn't see coming was the social side. Walking to the station every day, I've got to know half the street. We stop and chat, we have each other's keys now. When I drove, I didn't even know my next-door neighbour's name."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a man called Felix Grant, who illustrates children's books.",script:[["F","My guest today is Felix Grant, whose drawings are in more than forty children's books. Felix, were you always drawing as a child?"],["M","Constantly, but I wasn't especially good. My older sister was the talented one. What I had was stubbornness – I'd draw the same dog fifty times until it looked right. My parents still have a box of those dogs in the loft."],["F","How did you get your first book?"],["M","Through a competition I almost didn't enter. A publisher was looking for new illustrators, and I sent in three pictures the night before the deadline. I didn't win – I came fourth – but one of the judges remembered my work and rang me a year later when she needed someone for a book about a lost penguin."],["F","When you get a new story, where do you start?"],["M","People assume I start with the main character, but I don't. I start with the places – the kitchen, the forest, the street. If I know exactly what the world looks like, the characters seem to walk into it naturally. If I start with the characters, they end up floating on white paper."],["F","Do you work closely with the authors?"],["M","Less than you'd think. Usually the publisher keeps us apart until quite late, deliberately. The idea is that the pictures should tell their own part of the story, not just repeat the words. Some of my favourite books are ones where the picture shows something the text doesn't mention at all."],["F","Do you test your drawings on children?"],["M","Not officially, but my nephew is a very honest critic. He's seven. If he's bored, he turns the page before I've finished explaining. Adults are polite; children aren't, and that's far more useful."],["F","Has the job changed since you started?"],["M","Enormously. I used to post original paintings to the publisher in a big flat box and pray they arrived. Now nearly everything is digital, which is faster – but I still start every book with a pencil, because that's where the ideas come from. The screen is for finishing."],["F","What advice would you give to young people who want to illustrate books?"],["M","Draw from real life, not from other people's drawings. Go to a café, a bus stop, and draw the people there. You'll learn more about how bodies move in an afternoon than in a month of copying."]],qs:[{q:"What does Felix say about himself as a child?",o:["He was more talented than his sister.","He was very determined.","He wanted to become an illustrator."],a:1,x:"‘What I had was stubbornness – I'd draw the same dog fifty times until it looked right.’ His sister was the talented one."},{q:"How did Felix get his first book?",o:["He won a competition.","A judge contacted him later.","He sent his work to many publishers."],a:1,x:"‘I came fourth – but one of the judges remembered my work and rang me a year later’"},{q:"When Felix starts a new book, he begins by drawing",o:["the settings.","the main character.","the cover."],a:0,x:"‘I start with the places – the kitchen, the forest, the street.’"},{q:"Why do publishers keep authors and illustrators apart?",o:["so that the pictures add something of their own","because authors often disagree with illustrators","to save time during the project"],a:0,x:"‘the pictures should tell their own part of the story, not just repeat the words’"},{q:"Why does Felix value his nephew's opinion?",o:["He is an experienced reader.","He is the same age as the readers.","He shows honestly when he isn't interested."],a:2,x:"‘If he's bored, he turns the page… Adults are polite; children aren't, and that's far more useful.’"},{q:"What does Felix say about working digitally?",o:["It has made the work less creative.","He has stopped using paint completely.","He prefers to begin with traditional methods."],a:2,x:"‘I still start every book with a pencil, because that's where the ideas come from. The screen is for finishing.’"},{q:"What advice does Felix give to young illustrators?",o:["to draw people they see around them","to copy the work of artists they admire","to study at an art school"],a:0,x:"‘Draw from real life, not from other people's drawings. Go to a café, a bus stop, and draw the people there.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.listening.push({id:"l8",title:"Listening Test 8",topics:"Everyday situations · Volunteering at a dark-sky park · The best advice I was given · A football referee",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a flat he has looked at.",q:"What is he unsure about?",o:["the rent","the location","the size of the rooms"],a:2,x:"‘I just don't know if my desk and a bed would both fit in that second room’. The rent is ‘fair for the area’ and the location is ‘perfect’.",script:[["M","The rent's fair for the area – I'd checked a few others, and it's about average. And the location's perfect, five minutes from the station. I just don't know if my desk and a bed would both fit in that second room. The photos made it look bigger. I've asked if I can go back with a tape measure before I decide."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a wedding they went to.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The food was disappointing.","The speeches went on too long.","The music was too loud."],a:1,x:"She says ‘forty minutes of speeches’ and he agrees: ‘I thought they'd never end’. He liked the food, and she liked the band.",script:[["F","Lovely day, wasn't it? Though forty minutes of speeches before anyone could eat…"],["M","Honestly, I thought they'd never end. The best man alone did fifteen minutes."],["F","And then the food was a bit small, I thought."],["M","I thought it was delicious. Just not enough of it, maybe."],["F","The band was brilliant, though. I danced till midnight."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman phoning a vet.",q:"Why is she calling?",o:["to make an appointment","to ask for some advice","to check the cost of a treatment"],a:1,x:"‘I don't think it's worth bringing him in yet… I just wanted to ask whether I should be worried’. She says she'll book if needed; cost isn't mentioned.",script:[["F","Hello, my name's Clare Hughes, my dog Barney is registered with you. He's eaten something in the park – a bit of bread, I think, possibly with some mould on it. He seems completely fine, and I don't think it's worth bringing him in yet. I just wanted to ask whether I should be worried, and what signs to look out for. If he needs to be seen, I'll book, of course."]]},{ctx:"You hear a girl talking about acting in a school play.",q:"How did she feel after the first night?",o:["relieved that it went well","disappointed with her performance","nervous about the next performance"],a:0,x:"‘when the curtain came down I just felt this huge weight lift’. She forgot one line but ‘nobody noticed’, and she's ‘looking forward to’ the next night.",script:[["F","I'd been dreading it for weeks – I've never been on a stage before. And I did forget one line in the second act, but my friend covered for me and nobody noticed. When the curtain came down I just felt this huge weight lift. It had actually gone well. I'm looking forward to Friday now, which I never thought I'd say."]]},{ctx:"You hear a head teacher speaking at a school assembly.",q:"What is she mainly doing?",o:["announcing a new rule","congratulating some students","asking students to help with an event"],a:1,x:"‘I want to say how proud we are of the debating team, who won the regional final on Saturday’ – the whole talk praises them. The concert and the rule about phones are mentioned only briefly.",script:[["F","Good morning, everyone. Before the usual notices, I want to say how proud we are of the debating team, who won the regional final on Saturday against schools twice our size. They practised every lunchtime for two months, and it showed. Please give them a round of applause. The reminder about phones in lessons and the details of next week's concert are on the noticeboard."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a guided walk around a city.",q:"What did she enjoy most?",o:["the guide's stories","the buildings","the other people in the group"],a:0,x:"‘it was the stories that made it – who'd lived in which house, who'd fallen out with whom’. The buildings were ‘just buildings’ without them; the group was ‘quiet’.",script:[["F","I'd walked down those streets a hundred times, and honestly, the buildings were just buildings to me. The group was quite quiet, mostly tourists. But it was the stories that made it – who'd lived in which house, who'd fallen out with whom, where the old market used to be. The guide made the whole place come alive. I've booked the evening one now."]]},{ctx:"You hear two people talking about a restaurant booking.",q:"What is the problem?",o:["The restaurant is fully booked.","They booked for the wrong evening.","The restaurant has closed early."],a:1,x:"‘You booked it for the fourteenth. It's the fifteenth today.’ The restaurant has a table but not until nine, so it isn't full or closed.",script:[["M","They're saying they haven't got a table for us."],["F","But I booked weeks ago. Let me see the email. Oh no."],["M","What?"],["F","You booked it for the fourteenth. It's the fifteenth today."],["M","Ah. Well, they said they could fit us in at nine, if we don't mind waiting."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about his vegetable garden.",q:"Why did he start growing vegetables?",o:["to spend more time outdoors","to save money on food","to teach his children where food comes from"],a:2,x:"‘My daughter thought carrots came from a plastic bag… I wanted the kids to see things actually grow’. Being outdoors and saving money came later or not at all.",script:[["M","People assume it's about saving money, but honestly, by the time you've bought seeds and tools and a water butt, you're not saving much. It started because my daughter thought carrots came from a plastic bag, and I wanted the kids to see things actually grow. The fresh air is a bonus – I'm out there most evenings now."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a man called Rhys Morgan talking about volunteering at a dark-sky park.",script:[["M",`Good evening. I'm Rhys Morgan, and I volunteer at a dark-sky park in Wales – that's an area with so little artificial light that on a clear night you can see the Milky Way with your own eyes.

I got involved by accident. I was a primary school teacher, and I took a class there on a trip. I'd planned to show them the stars for half an hour, but it was so beautiful that we stayed until the coach driver lost patience. The following week I signed up as a volunteer.

What do I actually do? Mostly I run evening sessions for visitors. The most popular one is for families, and it's held in a field next to an old farmhouse that the park uses as its centre.

The first thing I tell people is to put their phones away. Your eyes need about twenty minutes to adjust to the dark, and one look at a bright screen sends you back to the start. We give everyone a torch with a red light instead, because red light doesn't ruin your night vision.

People expect the telescope to be the highlight, but actually the thing that impresses them most is a simple pair of binoculars. Through those, you suddenly see thousands of stars where there seemed to be a few hundred.

The biggest enemy of our work isn't light from the towns any more, because the councils have been very helpful. It's cloud. Wales is not famous for clear skies, and we cancel roughly half of our sessions.

If I had to choose one favourite sight, it would be Saturn. When children see the rings through the telescope for the first time, they always think it's a picture stuck on the lens.

On the best night I've ever had there, we saw a meteor shower – more than sixty shooting stars in an hour. Nobody said a word for the whole time.

If you'd like to visit, my advice is to go in winter, not summer. The nights are longer, so you don't have to stay up until midnight, and the air is often clearer too.`]],items:[{s:"On a clear night at the park, you can see the {g} without a telescope.",a:["Milky Way"],x:"‘you can see the Milky Way with your own eyes’"},{s:"Rhys used to work as a {g}.",a:["primary school teacher","teacher","school teacher"],x:"‘I was a primary school teacher’"},{s:"The family sessions are held in a field next to an old {g}.",a:["farmhouse"],x:"‘held in a field next to an old farmhouse that the park uses as its centre’"},{s:"Rhys says your eyes need about {g} to get used to the dark.",a:["twenty minutes","20 minutes"],x:"‘Your eyes need about twenty minutes to adjust to the dark’"},{s:"Visitors are given a torch with a {g} light.",a:["red"],x:"‘We give everyone a torch with a red light instead’"},{s:"Visitors are most impressed by looking through {g}.",a:["binoculars","a pair of binoculars"],x:"‘the thing that impresses them most is a simple pair of binoculars’. The telescope is the distractor."},{s:"The biggest problem for the park now is {g}.",a:["cloud","clouds"],x:"‘The biggest enemy of our work isn't light from the towns any more… It's cloud.’"},{s:"Rhys's favourite thing to show visitors is {g}.",a:["Saturn"],x:"‘If I had to choose one favourite sight, it would be Saturn.’"},{s:"On his best night, Rhys saw over {g} shooting stars in an hour.",a:["sixty","60"],x:"‘more than sixty shooting stars in an hour’"},{s:"Rhys recommends visiting the park in {g}.",a:["winter"],x:"‘my advice is to go in winter, not summer’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about the best advice they were ever given.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about the advice. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I ignored it at first.","It came from someone I hardly knew.","It came from one of my teachers.","I've passed it on to my own children.","It made me change my job.","I didn't understand it until much later.","It stopped me spending money foolishly.","It was given to me as a joke."],speakers:[{a:1,x:"B: ‘a stranger on a train… I never even got her name’. She uses it at work, but didn't change her job (E).",script:[["F","It was a stranger on a train, of all people. I was on my way to my first proper job interview, practically shaking, and the woman opposite said, ‘They want you to be good, you know. They're on your side.’ I never even got her name. I've used that thought before every meeting and presentation since."]]},{a:5,x:"F: ‘I honestly had no idea what it meant… It was twenty years before I understood’. He didn't ignore it – he repeated it to his friends (A).",script:[["M","My grandfather used to say, ‘Never be the cleverest person in the room.’ As a teenager I used to repeat it to my friends because it sounded wise, but I honestly had no idea what it meant – why would you want to be less clever? It was twenty years before I understood: if you are, you've stopped learning. Now I go looking for rooms full of people who know more than me."]]},{a:6,x:"G: ‘wait a week before you buy anything over fifty pounds… I've saved a fortune’. Her husband finds the rule annoying; nothing is said about children (D).",script:[["F","My first boss told me: wait a week before you buy anything over fifty pounds. If you still want it in a week, buy it. I was terrible with money at the time – I'd see a coat and just buy it. Since then, I reckon I've saved a fortune on things I'd have worn once. My husband finds the rule very annoying."]]},{a:0,x:"A: ‘I didn't listen, of course – I was nineteen. It took failing my driving test twice… before I started taking it seriously.’ The advice came from his mum, not a teacher (C).",script:[["M","My mum always told me to sleep on big decisions and never to do anything important when I was tired. I didn't listen, of course – I was nineteen. It took failing my driving test twice, both times after staying up all night, before I started taking it seriously. Third time, I slept nine hours and passed easily."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘I've said the same thing to both my boys’. It came from her father, whom she knew well (B).",script:[["F","My father said, ‘Be the person who says thank you.’ Simple as that. Thank the bus driver, thank the cleaner, write the card. He said people forget what you did but remember how you made them feel. I've said the same thing to both my boys, probably a thousand times. I hope some of it has gone in."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a woman called Naomi Brooks, who is a professional football referee.",script:[["M","Joining me is Naomi Brooks, who referees in the national women's league. Naomi, how did you start?"],["F","I played until I was twenty, quite seriously, but I was never going to be good enough for the top level. A coach suggested I try refereeing to stay in the game. I said no for a whole season – I thought referees were the enemy! Then I did one children's match as a favour, and I loved having the whole game in front of me."],["M","What's the physical side like?"],["F","People don't realise how fit you have to be. I run about eleven kilometres in a match, and I'm tested twice a year. But the part I find most tiring isn't the running, it's the concentration. Ninety minutes of making decisions with no break. After a big game I sleep for ten hours."],["M","How do you deal with players who disagree with you?"],["F","Early on, I'd argue back, and that only makes things worse. Now I try to explain very briefly – one sentence – and walk away. Players mostly want to feel they've been heard. What I won't accept is a group surrounding me; that's when I'll reach for a card."],["M","Has video technology changed your job?"],["F","We use it in some competitions, and it does catch mistakes. The downside nobody talks about is that it changes how you referee. There's a temptation to leave the decision to the screen. I try to make every call as if the cameras weren't there."],["M","What about criticism – on social media, for example?"],["F","I don't read it. Not on match days, not ever. My partner sometimes tells me if something's being said that I need to know, but otherwise I'd rather watch the game back myself and judge my own performance. I'm my own harshest critic anyway."],["M","What's the best moment you've had?"],["F","Last season's cup final, without a doubt – but not for the reason you'd think. It wasn't the crowd or the trophy. It was that afterwards, both captains came to shake my hand and nobody was talking about the referee at all. That's the perfect game for us."],["M","And your advice for young people thinking about refereeing?"],["F","Start with children's football on Saturday mornings. The pressure's low, the parents are mostly friendly, and you'll learn the basics of positioning before anyone's shouting at you."]],qs:[{q:"Why did Naomi start refereeing?",o:["She was too injured to continue playing.","She enjoyed a match she agreed to referee.","A coach persuaded her immediately."],a:1,x:"‘I did one children's match as a favour, and I loved having the whole game in front of me.’ She refused the coach's suggestion ‘for a whole season’."},{q:"What does Naomi find most tiring about refereeing?",o:["the amount of running","the fitness tests","having to concentrate for so long"],a:2,x:"‘the part I find most tiring isn't the running, it's the concentration’"},{q:"How does Naomi deal with players who disagree with her now?",o:["She gives a short explanation.","She shows them a card straight away.","She lets them argue until they calm down."],a:0,x:"‘Now I try to explain very briefly – one sentence – and walk away.’ A card is only for a group surrounding her."},{q:"What worries Naomi about video technology?",o:["It makes too many mistakes.","Referees may rely on it too much.","It slows the game down for fans."],a:1,x:"‘There's a temptation to leave the decision to the screen.’"},{q:"How does Naomi respond to criticism on social media?",o:["She avoids reading it.","She asks her partner to reply.","She reads it after big matches."],a:0,x:"‘I don't read it. Not on match days, not ever.’"},{q:"Why was the cup final Naomi's best moment?",o:["because of the size of the crowd","because she was praised in the media","because nobody discussed her decisions"],a:2,x:"‘nobody was talking about the referee at all. That's the perfect game for us.’"},{q:"Why does Naomi recommend starting with children's football?",o:["It is easier to learn positioning there.","Children respect referees more.","There are more matches to choose from."],a:0,x:"‘The pressure's low… and you'll learn the basics of positioning before anyone's shouting at you.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.plistening.push({id:"pl1",practice:!0,title:"Practice set 1",topics:"Everyday situations · Training guide dogs · A favourite room · A theatre lighting designer",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a man phoning a lost property office.",q:"What has he lost?",o:["a bag","a coat","a pair of glasses"],a:1,x:"‘It's a long grey coat… my glasses were in the pocket, but I've found those’. The bag is mentioned as what he thought he'd lost at first.",script:[["M","Hello, I was on the 8.15 from Leeds this morning, and I think I left something on the train. At first I thought it was my bag, but no, that's here with me. It's a long grey coat, quite new, with a blue lining. My glasses were in the pocket, but I've found those – they'd fallen into my bag. Could you call me if it's handed in?"]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends planning a surprise party.",q:"What do they disagree about?",o:["where to hold it","who to invite","when to have it"],a:0,x:"He wants the flat, she says ‘there's no way we'll fit thirty people in your flat’ and suggests the café. They agree on Saturday and the guest list.",script:[["F","So, Saturday the twelfth – we're agreed on that?"],["M","Definitely. And we invite everyone from the running club, plus her family."],["F","Fine. But there's no way we'll fit thirty people in your flat."],["M","We managed for my birthday."],["F","Barely. I still think the back room of the café would be better."]]},{ctx:"You hear a teacher talking to a class.",q:"Why is she talking to them?",o:["to remind them about a deadline","to tell them about a change of room","to ask for volunteers"],a:2,x:"‘I need four of you to help show the new students round on Thursday – hands up if you can.’ The room and the project deadline are mentioned only briefly.",script:[["F","Right, before we start – your projects are due on Friday, as you know, and we're in room twelve again next week. But the real reason I wanted a word: I need four of you to help show the new students round on Thursday morning. You'll miss the first lesson, which I know will break your hearts. Hands up if you can."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a new hairdresser.",q:"What does she like most about the hairdresser?",o:["the price","the advice she was given","the friendly atmosphere"],a:1,x:"‘she told me honestly that the style I'd asked for wouldn't suit my hair, and suggested something else – she was right’. The price is ‘about the same’, the salon ‘a bit quiet’.",script:[["F","It's about the same price as my old place, so that's not why I'm going back. And the salon itself is a bit quiet – no music, no chatting. But she told me honestly that the style I'd asked for wouldn't suit my hair, and suggested something else. I wasn't sure, but she was right. Nobody's ever done that before."]]},{ctx:"You hear two students talking about a quiz night.",q:"How does the woman feel about the result?",o:["proud of her team","annoyed with one of the questions","surprised that they did so well"],a:2,x:"‘I honestly thought we'd come last… second out of twelve!’ He is the one who complains about a question.",script:[["M","I still say the capital of Australia question was unfair."],["F","It was a perfectly fair question; you just got it wrong. Anyway, who cares? I honestly thought we'd come last. Second out of twelve!"],["M","I suppose. If I'd said Canberra we'd have won."],["F","Let it go."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a message for a gym.",q:"What does he want to do?",o:["cancel his membership","change the type of membership","complain about a class"],a:1,x:"‘I'd like to switch to the off-peak membership instead of the full one.’ He says he isn't leaving and the classes are ‘great’.",script:[["M","Hi, it's Ravi Shah, membership number 2-0-7-7. I'm not leaving, don't worry – the classes are great. It's just that my working hours have changed, so I can only come during the day now. I'd like to switch to the off-peak membership instead of the full one, from next month if possible. Thanks."]]},{ctx:"You hear a radio presenter talking about the weather.",q:"What is she mainly doing?",o:["warning people about travel problems","describing yesterday's weather","giving advice about the heat"],a:0,x:"‘the fog… means flights are delayed and the motorway is down to one lane… leave extra time’. Yesterday's sunshine and the heat later are mentioned only briefly.",script:[["F","After yesterday's sunshine, it's a very different picture this morning. Thick fog has come in overnight, and that means flights from the airport are delayed by at least two hours, and the motorway is down to one lane near the bridge. If you're travelling, please leave extra time. It should clear by lunchtime, and it'll be warm again by the afternoon."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about learning to draw online.",q:"What does he say about learning online?",o:["He prefers it to having lessons in person.","It has helped him improve quickly.","He finds it difficult to concentrate."],a:1,x:"‘I've improved more in six months online than in years at evening classes’. He says he still prefers a real teacher in the room.",script:[["M","I'll be honest, I still prefer a real teacher in the room, looking over my shoulder. But I've improved more in six months online than in years at evening classes. You can watch a lesson as many times as you like, pause it, and copy every line exactly. And you can do it at midnight if you want to. It's a bit addictive, actually."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Laura Ngata talking about her work training guide dogs.",script:[["F",`Hello. I'm Laura Ngata, and I train guide dogs – the dogs that help blind and partially sighted people to move around safely. I've been doing it for twelve years. Before that I worked in a bank, and the only dog I'd ever owned was a very badly behaved terrier.

People imagine I train puppies, but the puppies don't come to me straight away. For their first year, they live with volunteer families who teach them basic manners and take them everywhere – on buses, into shops, to the cinema – so that nothing frightens them later.

Most of our dogs are labradors, or a cross between a labrador and a golden retriever. They're calm, clever and, most importantly, they love food, which makes them easy to reward.

The training itself takes about six months. The hardest skill to teach is something we call intelligent disobedience. If the owner tells the dog to cross the road, but there's a car coming, the dog has to refuse. Teaching a dog when not to obey is far more difficult than teaching it to obey.

Not every dog makes it. About one in three are changed to a different career, usually because they're too easily distracted. Those dogs often become therapy dogs in hospitals, or go back to live with the families who raised them.

Matching a dog with an owner is the part of my job I love most. It isn't just about walking speed, although that matters. We look at the person's lifestyle – whether they live in a busy city or a quiet village, how much they travel – and find the dog whose personality suits them.

The first walk together is always emotional. I remember one man who hadn't been out on his own for four years. He walked to the end of his street and back, and then he just stood there and laughed.

A guide dog usually works for about eight years before it retires. After that, many owners keep their dog as a pet, even when a new guide dog arrives.

If you'd like to help, the easiest way is to become a puppy-raising volunteer. You don't need any experience – just patience, a sense of humour and plenty of time.`]],items:[{s:"Before training guide dogs, Laura worked in a {g}.",a:["bank"],x:"‘Before that I worked in a bank’"},{s:"For their first year, the puppies live with volunteer {g}.",a:["families"],x:"‘they live with volunteer families who teach them basic manners’"},{s:"Laura says the dogs are easy to reward because they love {g}.",a:["food"],x:"‘they love food, which makes them easy to reward’"},{s:"Training a guide dog takes about {g}.",a:["six months","6 months"],x:"‘The training itself takes about six months.’"},{s:"The most difficult skill to teach is called intelligent {g}.",a:["disobedience"],x:"‘something we call intelligent disobedience’"},{s:"Dogs that don't finish the training are usually too easily {g}.",a:["distracted"],x:"‘usually because they're too easily distracted’"},{s:"Some of these dogs become therapy dogs in {g}.",a:["hospitals"],x:"‘Those dogs often become therapy dogs in hospitals’"},{s:"When matching dogs and owners, Laura considers the person's {g} as well as walking speed.",a:["lifestyle"],x:"‘We look at the person's lifestyle’"},{s:"One man had not been out alone for {g} before getting his dog.",a:["four years","4 years"],x:"‘who hadn't been out on his own for four years’"},{s:"Guide dogs normally work for around {g} before retiring.",a:["eight years","8 years"],x:"‘A guide dog usually works for about eight years before it retires.’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about their favourite room in their home.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about their favourite room. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["It reminds me of someone.","I designed it myself.","It's where the family meets.","It's the only quiet place in the house.","I use it for my hobby.","It has the best view.","I didn't like it at first.","It's the smallest room."],speakers:[{a:2,x:"C: ‘it's the one place where all five of us end up every evening’. The view is ‘nothing special’ (F).",script:[["F","It has to be the kitchen. There's a big old table that came with the house, and somehow it's the one place where all five of us end up every evening, even if we're all doing different things. The view from the window is nothing special – just the neighbours' fence – but I wouldn't swap that room for anything."]]},{a:6,x:"G: ‘I hated the green walls when we moved in… now I wouldn't change them’. Someone else chose the colour, so B is wrong.",script:[["M","When we moved in, I hated the living room. The previous owners had painted it a dark green, and I thought it looked like the inside of a cave. We kept meaning to repaint it and never got round to it. Now, ten years later, I wouldn't change it. In winter, with the fire on, it's the cosiest room I've ever sat in."]]},{a:0,x:"A: ‘it was my grandmother's sewing room… I can still smell her perfume’. She goes there to think – it isn't for a hobby (E).",script:[["F","My favourite room is the little one at the top of the stairs. It used to be my grandmother's sewing room – she lived with us for her last few years – and I've kept her chair and her lamp. Sometimes I think I can still smell her perfume. I go up there whenever I need to think something through. It feels like she's keeping me company."]]},{a:4,x:"E: ‘that's where I keep my model railway’. He says the garage is ‘freezing’ and ‘not quiet’, so D is wrong.",script:[["M","Officially it's the garage, but the car hasn't been in it for years, because that's where I keep my model railway. It runs all the way round the walls. It isn't quiet – the trains make a terrible noise – and it's freezing in winter, but I can spend a whole Sunday out there and not notice the time going by."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘with three teenagers… the bathroom is the only place where nobody bothers me’. It isn't the smallest – ‘surprisingly big’ (H).",script:[["F","This will sound strange, but it's the bathroom. With three teenagers in the house, it's the only place where nobody bothers me – they all have headphones in everywhere else, but the noise still somehow gets through. It's surprisingly big for an old house, and I've put a plant and a little radio in there. Twenty minutes in the bath and I'm a new person."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a man called Sam Okafor, who designs the lighting for theatre productions.",script:[["F","My guest today is Sam Okafor, who designs lighting for theatres. Sam, most people probably don't notice the lighting in a play. Is that a problem?"],["M","Not at all – in a way, that's the point. If the audience is thinking about the lights, I've probably done something wrong. The best lighting makes you feel something without you knowing why. You leave thinking it was a wonderful performance, and the lighting is part of that, quietly."],["F","How did you get into it?"],["M","Completely by accident. I wanted to be an actor, and at drama school I was – let's say – not the most talented student. But one evening the person operating the lights was ill, and I offered to do it. I realised I enjoyed that far more than being on stage. My acting teacher was very relieved."],["F","Where do you start when you design a show?"],["M","With the script, always, long before I see the set. I read it several times and make notes about the time of day in every scene, the weather, the mood. Then I talk to the director. Some directors have very clear ideas about light; others say, ‘You decide,’ which is exciting but slightly terrifying."],["F","Has technology changed the job?"],["M","Enormously. Twenty years ago, every light had to be pointed by hand from a ladder. Now most lights move by themselves, and I can programme hundreds of changes from a computer. The danger is that because you can do anything, you try to do everything. Some of the most powerful moments I've created used a single, simple light."],["F","What's the most difficult part?"],["M","Time. I usually only get a few days in the actual theatre before the first performance, and everyone needs the stage at once – the actors are rehearsing, the set is being finished. Often the only time I can work quietly is at three in the morning."],["F","What advice would you give someone who wants to do your job?"],["M","Look at real light, all the time. The way the sun comes through a window in the morning, the colour of a street lamp in the rain. I keep photos on my phone of light I've seen in ordinary places. Half my ideas come from a bus stop or a kitchen, not from other people's shows."]],qs:[{q:"What does Sam say about good theatre lighting?",o:["It should be noticed by the audience.","It affects the audience without them realising.","It is more important than the acting."],a:1,x:"‘The best lighting makes you feel something without you knowing why.’"},{q:"How did Sam begin working in lighting?",o:["He was asked by his acting teacher.","He took the place of someone who was unwell.","He studied it at drama school."],a:1,x:"‘the person operating the lights was ill, and I offered to do it’"},{q:"What is the first thing Sam does when designing a show?",o:["He studies the script in detail.","He looks at the design of the set.","He has a meeting with the director."],a:0,x:"‘With the script, always, long before I see the set.’ The director comes after."},{q:"How does Sam feel when a director leaves the decisions to him?",o:["confident","both excited and nervous","disappointed"],a:1,x:"‘which is exciting but slightly terrifying’"},{q:"What risk does Sam see in modern lighting technology?",o:["It breaks down too often.","It is too expensive for small theatres.","It can encourage designers to do too much."],a:2,x:"‘because you can do anything, you try to do everything’"},{q:"Why does Sam sometimes work at three in the morning?",o:["The theatre is too busy during the day.","He prefers working when it is quiet outside.","The lights work better at night."],a:0,x:"‘everyone needs the stage at once… the only time I can work quietly is at three in the morning’"},{q:"What advice does Sam give to future lighting designers?",o:["to watch as many shows as possible","to observe light in everyday life","to learn to programme computers"],a:1,x:"‘Look at real light, all the time… Half my ideas come from a bus stop or a kitchen, not from other people's shows.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.plistening.push({id:"pl2",practice:!0,title:"Practice set 2",topics:"Everyday situations · A weather forecaster · Getting lost · A crossword setter",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a concert she went to.",q:"What disappointed her?",o:["the length of the concert","the sound quality","the seats"],a:0,x:"‘they played for barely an hour… for that price I expected more’. The sound was ‘perfect’ and the seats were ‘fine’.",script:[["F","The sound was perfect – you could hear every word, which isn't always the case in that hall. Our seats were fine too, a bit high up but you could see everything. But they played for barely an hour, including the encore. For that price I expected more. I'd waited eight months for those tickets."]]},{ctx:"You hear two neighbours talking about a tree.",q:"What do they agree to do?",o:["share the cost of cutting it","ask the council for advice","leave it as it is"],a:0,x:"‘Let's split it, then – half each.’ ‘Deal.’ The council is suggested and rejected.",script:[["M","The branches are right over my garage now. One good storm and they'll come through the roof."],["F","I know, I've been meaning to do something. Should we ask the council?"],["M","They'll take months. A tree surgeon could do it next week, but it's three hundred pounds."],["F","Let's split it, then – half each. It's partly my fault for leaving it."],["M","Deal."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a message for a colleague.",q:"Why is he calling?",o:["to apologise for missing a meeting","to ask for some information","to change the time of a meeting"],a:1,x:"‘could you send me the figures from last month before two?’ He will still be at the meeting at three.",script:[["M","Hi Nadia, it's Pete. I'll definitely be at the meeting at three, don't worry. But I've realised I don't have the sales figures from last month, and I'll look a bit silly presenting without them. Could you send me the figures from last month before two? Just the summary is fine. Thanks, you're a star."]]},{ctx:"You hear a student talking about a part-time job in a cinema.",q:"What does he dislike about the job?",o:["the low pay","cleaning up after customers","working late at night"],a:1,x:"‘the worst thing is clearing up after a children's film – popcorn absolutely everywhere’. The pay is ‘fine for a student’ and he likes the late shifts.",script:[["M","The pay's fine for a student, and I actually like the late shifts – I'm a night person anyway, and I get to watch the end of films for free. The worst thing is clearing up after a children's film on a Saturday. Popcorn absolutely everywhere, and sticky drinks under every seat. It takes the four of us nearly an hour."]]},{ctx:"You hear a tour guide speaking to a group.",q:"What is she doing?",o:["describing the history of a building","giving safety instructions","explaining a change to the programme"],a:1,x:"‘please stay on the path… hold the rail on the stairs… don't lean over the wall’ – the whole message is about safety.",script:[["F","Before we go up to the top of the tower, a few important things. The stairs are steep and narrow, so please hold the rail at all times, and take your time. At the top, stay on the marked path, and please don't lean over the wall, however good the view is. And if anyone feels dizzy, just tell me and we'll come down together."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a cookery book.",q:"What does she say about it?",o:["The recipes are too complicated.","The photographs are misleading.","The instructions are very clear."],a:2,x:"‘every step is explained so clearly that even I couldn't get it wrong’. The photos are ‘nothing special’, the recipes ‘simple’.",script:[["F","I'm a terrible cook, so I was a bit worried, but every step is explained so clearly that even I couldn't get it wrong. The recipes are quite simple – nothing fancy – and the photographs are nothing special, to be honest. But I've made six things from it now, and they've all turned out exactly as they should."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a film.",q:"What does the man think of it?",o:["It was too long.","It was better than the book.","The ending was predictable."],a:1,x:"‘For once the film's actually better than the book’. She says it was long; he disagrees.",script:[["F","Three hours, though. I was losing the will to live by the end."],["M","I didn't notice the time at all. And for once the film's actually better than the book – they cut all the boring bits."],["F","Did you guess the ending?"],["M","No idea until the last minute. Did you?"],["F","No, I'll give it that."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a new phone app for buses.",q:"Why does he like it?",o:["It shows exactly when buses will arrive.","It lets him pay for tickets.","It suggests faster routes."],a:0,x:"‘you can see where your bus actually is, to the minute’. Paying is done ‘with my card anyway’; he doesn't mention routes.",script:[["M","I pay with my card on the bus anyway, so that part doesn't matter to me. What's brilliant is that you can see where your bus actually is, to the minute, on a map. I used to stand at the stop in the rain for twenty minutes. Now I leave the house exactly when I need to. It's changed my mornings."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Fiona Grant talking about her work as a television weather forecaster.",script:[["F",`Good evening, everyone. I'm Fiona Grant, and for the past fifteen years I've been presenting the weather on regional television. People are often surprised to hear that I'm a scientist first – I have a degree in physics and spent four years at the national weather service before I ever stood in front of a camera.

A forecast you see on television lasts about two minutes, but behind it there are hours of work. My day starts at six in the morning, when I look at data from satellites, weather balloons and thousands of stations on the ground.

The computer models do much of the calculating, but they don't always agree with each other. My job is to decide which one to trust. That's where experience really matters. I know, for example, that our local hills often stop rain arriving when the models say it will.

There's no script when I'm on air. Everything is in my head, and I talk to the camera while pointing at a screen that I can't actually see – I'm looking at a small monitor at the side. Learning to do that without looking lost took me nearly a year.

The question I'm asked most is whether I bring an umbrella. The answer is always yes. Even with the best forecast in the world, the weather can surprise you.

Viewers are very loyal, but they're also quick to complain. If I forecast sunshine and it rains on a wedding, I'll hear about it. The most letters I ever received were after a snowfall that I'd said would miss the city completely. I still remember that one.

The part of the job I enjoy most isn't the presenting – it's visiting schools to explain how weather works. Children ask far better questions than adults.

If you're interested in a career like mine, my advice is to study maths and science seriously first. The presenting can be learned later; the science can't be faked.`]],items:[{s:"Fiona has a university degree in {g}.",a:["physics"],x:"‘I have a degree in physics’"},{s:"Before presenting, Fiona worked for {g} at the national weather service.",a:["four years","4 years"],x:"‘spent four years at the national weather service’"},{s:"Fiona's working day begins at {g}.",a:["six","6","six o'clock","6 am"],x:"‘My day starts at six in the morning’"},{s:"Fiona says that local {g} often affect when rain arrives.",a:["hills"],x:"‘our local hills often stop rain arriving when the models say it will’"},{s:"Fiona doesn't use a {g} when she presents.",a:["script"],x:"‘There's no script when I'm on air.’"},{s:"While presenting, Fiona looks at a small {g} at the side.",a:["monitor"],x:"‘I'm looking at a small monitor at the side’"},{s:"It took Fiona almost {g} to learn to present without looking lost.",a:["a year","one year","1 year"],x:"‘Learning to do that without looking lost took me nearly a year.’"},{s:"Fiona received the most complaints after a forecast about {g}.",a:["snow","snowfall","a snowfall"],x:"‘after a snowfall that I'd said would miss the city completely’"},{s:"Fiona most enjoys visiting {g}.",a:["schools"],x:"‘The part of the job I enjoy most isn't the presenting – it's visiting schools’"},{s:"Fiona advises people to study maths and {g} first.",a:["science"],x:"‘study maths and science seriously first’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about a time when they got lost.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about getting lost. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I trusted technology too much.","A stranger helped me.","I ended up somewhere better than planned.","I was too proud to ask for directions.","I was frightened for the first time in years.","I missed something important.","I got lost because of the weather.","It's become a family joke."],speakers:[{a:0,x:"A: ‘I followed the satnav without looking… it took us onto a farm track’. Nobody helped them – they reversed out (B).",script:[["M","We were driving to a wedding in Wales, and I followed the satnav without once looking at a road sign. It took us down smaller and smaller lanes until we were on a farm track facing a gate and a field of cows. I'd have happily asked someone, but there was nobody around. I had to reverse half a mile. We did make the wedding, just, but I've checked the map before every trip since."]]},{a:2,x:"C: ‘we found this tiny beach that wasn't in any guidebook… the best day of the holiday’. They weren't scared (E).",script:[["F","We were trying to find a famous beach on the island and took a wrong turn somewhere. We weren't worried – it was a lovely day – and we just kept walking. Eventually we found this tiny beach that wasn't in any guidebook, with nobody else on it. We stayed there all afternoon. Honestly, it was the best day of the holiday."]]},{a:7,x:"H: ‘my kids still bring it up every Christmas’. He asked a newspaper seller straight away, so D is wrong.",script:[["M","I took the family to the zoo in a city I'd lived in for twenty years – except I drove us to the safari park on the other side of town, forty minutes in the wrong direction. When I realised, I asked the first man I saw, a newspaper seller, and he laughed at me. My kids still bring it up every Christmas, and my daughter always asks, very politely, whether I'd like her to check the map."]]},{a:5,x:"F: ‘by the time I found the hall, the exam had started… I had to resit it in the summer’. The weather was fine (G).",script:[["F","It was my first year at university, and the exam was in a building I'd never been to. It was a lovely sunny morning, so I decided to walk across the campus. Big mistake – all the buildings look the same. By the time I found the right hall, the exam had started and they wouldn't let me in. I had to resit it in the summer."]]},{a:4,x:"E: ‘I hadn't felt that kind of fear since I was a child’. The fog is mentioned, but she wasn't lost because of it – she'd left the path earlier (G).",script:[["F","I've walked in the mountains all my life, so I'm not easily scared. But that day I left the path to take a photo and couldn't find it again. Then the fog came down. I hadn't felt that kind of fear since I was a child. I sat still for an hour until it cleared, and I could see the path twenty metres away. I felt very foolish, and very lucky."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a man called Tom Ellery, who writes crosswords for newspapers.",script:[["F","My guest is Tom Ellery, whose crosswords appear in several national newspapers. Tom, how did you start?"],["M","My grandmother did the crossword every morning with a cup of tea, and when I was about nine she started reading me the clues. I was useless at first, but I was hooked. I sent my first crossword to a newspaper at sixteen. They sent it back with a very kind letter explaining everything that was wrong with it."],["F","How long does it take to write one?"],["M","Filling the grid with words is the quick part – maybe an hour. Writing the clues is what takes the time. A good clue has to be fair, so that when people finally get the answer, they think, ‘Of course!’ rather than ‘That's ridiculous.’ A full crossword usually takes me two days."],["F","Do you get feedback from solvers?"],["M","Constantly. People write to me, mostly to say a clue was impossible. Occasionally they're right, and I admit it. What I enjoy most, though, are the letters from people who solve the puzzle together – couples, families, groups of friends in a café. That's what crosswords are really for."],["F","Has the internet changed the job?"],["M","In some ways. Many people now solve on their phones, and they can check an answer instantly, which some setters see as cheating. I don't mind. If it helps someone finish a puzzle and come back the next day, that's a good thing. The bigger change is that young people are getting into crosswords again, through online communities."],["F","Is there any word you've never managed to fit into a crossword?"],["M","Hundreds! Long words with lots of unusual letters are very difficult. But the one I'm proudest of finally using is ‘rhythm’ – no vowels, apart from the y, and it took me years to find the right place for it."],["F","What's your advice for beginners who find crosswords too hard?"],["M","Start with the easiest one in the paper, and don't be afraid to look at the answers the next day. Work out why each answer is right. That's how you learn the tricks. Nobody's born knowing them – not even me."]],qs:[{q:"How did Tom become interested in crosswords?",o:["His grandmother shared her daily crossword with him.","A newspaper encouraged him to write one.","He was good at word games at school."],a:0,x:"‘she started reading me the clues… I was hooked’"},{q:"What happened when Tom first sent a crossword to a newspaper?",o:["It was published immediately.","It was rejected politely with advice.","He never received a reply."],a:1,x:"‘They sent it back with a very kind letter explaining everything that was wrong with it.’"},{q:"According to Tom, what makes a good clue?",o:["It is very difficult to solve.","It is short and simple.","It feels fair once it is solved."],a:2,x:'‘A good clue has to be fair… they think, "Of course!"’'},{q:"What does Tom enjoy most about solvers' letters?",o:["hearing about people solving together","learning about mistakes in his clues","receiving praise for difficult puzzles"],a:0,x:"‘What I enjoy most… are the letters from people who solve the puzzle together’"},{q:"What is Tom's attitude to people checking answers online?",o:["He thinks it spoils the challenge.","He accepts it if it keeps people solving.","He thinks newspapers should prevent it."],a:1,x:"‘I don't mind. If it helps someone finish a puzzle and come back the next day, that's a good thing.’"},{q:"Why is Tom proud of using the word ‘rhythm’?",o:["It was suggested by a reader.","It is his favourite word.","It was very hard to fit into a puzzle."],a:2,x:"‘it took me years to find the right place for it’"},{q:"What does Tom advise beginners to do?",o:["study the answers to understand them","only try very difficult puzzles","solve crosswords with other people"],a:0,x:"‘don't be afraid to look at the answers the next day. Work out why each answer is right.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.plistening.push({id:"pl3",practice:!0,title:"Practice set 3",topics:"Everyday situations · A penguin keeper · Performing in public · A professional storyteller",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a new sofa.",q:"What is she unhappy about?",o:["the colour","the delivery","the comfort"],a:1,x:"‘they turned up four hours late and left it in the hall’. She loves the colour and says it's ‘really comfortable’.",script:[["F","I love the colour – it's exactly the green I wanted – and it's really comfortable, which isn't always true of the nice-looking ones. But the delivery was a disaster. They turned up four hours late, and then said they weren't allowed to carry it upstairs, so they left it in the hall. My neighbour and I had to do it ourselves."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a cooking class.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The teacher was very good.","The class was too expensive.","The food was disappointing."],a:0,x:"‘She explained everything so clearly’ – ‘She did, didn't she?’. He thinks the price was fair; she liked the food.",script:[["F","I thought the teacher was brilliant. She explained everything so clearly."],["M","She did, didn't she? I actually understood what I was doing for once."],["F","Forty pounds is a lot, though."],["M","For three hours and all that food? I thought it was fair."],["F","Well, the curry was delicious, I'll give you that."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man leaving a message for a neighbour.",q:"Why is he calling?",o:["to ask her to look after his cat","to apologise for some noise","to tell her about a parcel"],a:2,x:"‘a parcel for you was left with me by mistake… it's on my doorstep’. The cat is mentioned only to say it's fine.",script:[["M","Hi Mrs Hall, it's Dan from number eight. Just to let you know, a parcel for you was left with me by mistake this morning. I've got to go out now, so I've put it on my doorstep, under the bench, out of the rain. Oh, and your cat's been sitting in my garden all day again – he's fine, he just likes the sun. See you soon."]]},{ctx:"You hear a student talking about a school trip.",q:"What did she enjoy most?",o:["the museum visit","the boat trip","the free time in the city"],a:2,x:"‘the best bit was the afternoon when they just let us explore on our own’. The museum was ‘a bit long’ and the boat ‘cold’.",script:[["F","The museum was interesting, but a bit long – three hours of paintings is a lot. The boat trip would have been great if it hadn't been so cold. But the best bit was the afternoon when they just let us explore on our own. We found this tiny market and ate the strangest cakes I've ever seen. I'd go back just for that."]]},{ctx:"You hear a coach talking to a team.",q:"What is he mainly doing?",o:["criticising the team's performance","praising the team's attitude","explaining a new tactic"],a:1,x:"‘we lost, but I've never been prouder of the way you kept going’ – the whole talk praises effort. Tactics are for ‘next week’.",script:[["M","Right, sit down for a minute. We lost, and I know that hurts. But I've never been prouder of the way you kept going when you were two goals down and everyone else had given up on us. That attitude is worth more than any result. We'll look at the tactics next week. Today, just be proud of yourselves."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about learning to drive.",q:"How does she feel now?",o:["confident about her test","nervous about driving on motorways","frustrated by her instructor"],a:0,x:"‘I honestly think I'll pass next week’. Motorways were the problem ‘at first’; her instructor is ‘very patient’.",script:[["F","At first motorways terrified me – all that speed – but after a few lessons I got used to it. My instructor is very patient, which helps. And now, I honestly think I'll pass next week. Six months ago I wouldn't have believed it. My only worry is parking, but I've been practising in the supermarket car park on Sundays."]]},{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about a new manager.",q:"What does the woman think of him?",o:["He is too strict.","He listens to staff.","He makes decisions too quickly."],a:1,x:"‘He actually asked what we thought before changing anything’. The man calls him strict; she disagrees.",script:[["M","He seems a bit strict, doesn't he? All those new rules about meetings."],["F","I don't think so. He actually asked what we thought before changing anything. The last manager never did that."],["M","I suppose. He takes ages to decide anything, though."],["F","Better than deciding everything in five minutes."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a hotel he stayed in.",q:"Why will he return?",o:["the location","the staff","the price"],a:1,x:"‘the staff remembered my name from the first day’. The location was ‘inconvenient’ and it wasn't cheap.",script:[["M","To be honest, the location wasn't very convenient – it was a twenty-minute bus ride into town – and it wasn't cheap either. But the staff were wonderful. They remembered my name from the first day, and when I mentioned it was my wife's birthday, there was a cake waiting in our room. That's why we'll definitely go back."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a man called Gareth Lloyd talking about his work as a penguin keeper at a zoo.",script:[["M",`Hello, everyone. I'm Gareth Lloyd, and I look after the penguins at a zoo in the north of England. We have forty-two of them, and yes, I know every single one by name. People ask how I can tell them apart. The answer is the pattern of black spots on their chests – every penguin's is different, like a fingerprint.

I didn't plan to work with penguins. I studied marine biology and expected to end up on a research boat. A summer job at the zoo turned into a permanent position, and that was eleven years ago.

My day starts at half past seven with the first feed. Penguins eat fish – mostly herring and sprats – and each bird gets around half a kilo a day. We feed them by hand, which lets us check how much each one eats. A penguin that suddenly stops eating is often the first sign that something is wrong.

The pool is cleaned every single morning. People are often surprised by how much effort that takes, but penguins are messy animals, and the water has to be perfect.

Our penguins come from South Africa, not the Antarctic, so they're perfectly happy in our climate. In fact, the biggest problem we have in summer isn't heat but sunburn – they have bare skin around their eyes, and on very sunny days we provide extra shade.

The breeding season is my favourite time of year. The pairs stay together for many years, and they return to the same nest box every spring. Last year we had nine chicks, which was a record for us.

The question I'm asked most often is whether penguins have personalities. They definitely do. One of our older birds, called Dot, follows me around during cleaning and tries to steal my brush every single day.

If you'd like to work with animals, my advice is to volunteer as early as you can. Most keepers I know started by cleaning out cages at weekends, and experience counts for more than anything you can learn from a book.`]],items:[{s:"There are {g} penguins at the zoo where Gareth works.",a:["forty-two","42","forty two"],x:"‘We have forty-two of them’"},{s:"Gareth identifies each penguin by the pattern of spots on its {g}.",a:["chest","chests"],x:"‘the pattern of black spots on their chests’"},{s:"Gareth studied {g} at university.",a:["marine biology"],x:"‘I studied marine biology’"},{s:"Each penguin eats about {g} of fish a day.",a:["half a kilo","0.5 kilos","half a kilogram"],x:"‘each bird gets around half a kilo a day’"},{s:"If a penguin stops {g}, it may be a sign of a problem.",a:["eating"],x:"‘A penguin that suddenly stops eating is often the first sign’"},{s:"The penguins' pool is cleaned every {g}.",a:["morning","day"],x:"‘The pool is cleaned every single morning.’"},{s:"The zoo's penguins originally come from {g}.",a:["South Africa"],x:"‘Our penguins come from South Africa, not the Antarctic’"},{s:"In summer, the main problem for the penguins is {g}.",a:["sunburn"],x:"‘the biggest problem we have in summer isn't heat but sunburn’"},{s:"Last year the zoo had a record number of {g} chicks.",a:["nine","9"],x:"‘Last year we had nine chicks, which was a record’"},{s:"A penguin called Dot tries to steal Gareth's {g}.",a:["brush"],x:"‘tries to steal my brush every single day’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about performing in public for the first time.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about the experience. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["I forgot what I was supposed to say.","I only agreed to do it to help someone.","A member of the audience made it easier.","I was surprised to enjoy it.","I prepared far more than necessary.","Something went wrong with the equipment.","I have never done it again.","My family didn't know I was doing it."],speakers:[{a:1,x:"B: ‘the band's singer had lost her voice… my sister begged me’. She wasn't surprised to enjoy it – she ‘hated every second’ (D).",script:[["F","My sister was in a band, and on the night of their biggest concert, the singer lost her voice. I'm the only other person who knew the words, so my sister begged me. I hated every second of it, honestly – the lights, the noise. But I couldn't let her down. I don't think I've ever been so glad when a song ended."]]},{a:5,x:"F: ‘the microphone stopped working halfway through’. He didn't forget his words – he finished without the microphone (A).",script:[["M","It was a poetry evening at a café, and I'd learnt my poem perfectly. Then halfway through, the microphone stopped working. There was this horrible silence. So I just stepped forward and shouted the rest. People said afterwards it was the most memorable performance of the night, but I'm not sure they meant it as a compliment."]]},{a:2,x:"C: ‘an old man in the front row smiled and nodded the whole time… I just spoke to him’. She prepared normally (E).",script:[["F","My first speech was at my grandfather's eightieth birthday. I was shaking so much I could hardly hold my notes. Then I noticed an old man in the front row – one of Grandad's friends – who smiled and nodded the whole time, as if everything I said was wonderful. So I just spoke to him, and it was fine."]]},{a:7,x:"H: ‘I didn't tell my parents… they found out from a neighbour’. He's done it ‘every month since’, so G is wrong.",script:[["M","I signed up for a comedy night at a pub without telling anyone. I didn't tell my parents because I knew they'd want to come, and I couldn't have stood that. They found out a week later from a neighbour who'd been in the audience. Mum was hurt, I think. Anyway, I've done it every month since, and now they come to every show."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘I was dreading it… but by the end I didn't want to get off the stage’. She was well prepared, but she doesn't say too much (E).",script:[["F","My dance school made everyone perform at the end-of-year show, and I was dreading it for months. I'm not a show-off at all. But when the music started, something happened. I stopped thinking and just danced, and by the end I didn't want to get off the stage. I'd never have believed I could feel like that."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a woman called Mira Sandhu, who works as a professional storyteller.",script:[["M","My guest today is Mira Sandhu, who earns her living telling stories – in schools, libraries and at festivals. Mira, what exactly does a storyteller do?"],["F","I tell stories out loud, from memory, to live audiences. No book, no screen. People sometimes think I'm an actor, but it's different. An actor stays inside the story; I keep stepping out of it to look at the audience and adjust what I'm doing depending on how they react."],["M","How did you start?"],["F","My grandmother told me stories every night when I was a child, mostly from India, where she grew up. When she died, I realised that if I didn't learn them, they would disappear. So I started telling them to my friends' children, and then at the local library, and gradually it became a job."],["M","Do you change the stories?"],["F","All the time. A story is never told the same way twice. With a group of five-year-olds, a story might take ten minutes; with teenagers, the same story might last forty, because they want more detail and more argument. The skeleton stays the same, but everything else moves."],["M","Which audiences are the most difficult?"],["F","People expect me to say teenagers, but actually they're often the best listeners once they decide you're worth listening to. The most difficult are adults at parties, who are polite but secretly checking their phones. Children are completely honest – if they're bored, they'll tell you."],["M","Has technology made your job harder?"],["F","I thought it would, but it's had the opposite effect. Because people spend so much time looking at screens, sitting in a room and just listening to someone has become unusual, almost a treat. Many teachers book me precisely because they want their students to practise listening."],["M","What advice would you give to someone who wants to tell stories?"],["F","Don't learn the words. Learn the pictures. If you can see the story clearly in your head, you'll find the words each time. People who learn a story word for word panic the moment they forget a sentence."]],qs:[{q:"How does Mira say her work differs from acting?",o:["She performs without any preparation.","She responds to the audience while she performs.","She only works with children."],a:1,x:"‘I keep stepping out of it to look at the audience and adjust what I'm doing’"},{q:"Why did Mira start learning stories?",o:["to keep her grandmother's stories alive","to entertain her own children","to get a job at a library"],a:0,x:"‘if I didn't learn them, they would disappear’"},{q:"What does Mira say about telling the same story to different groups?",o:["She always uses exactly the same words.","She chooses different stories for teenagers.","She changes the length and detail."],a:2,x:"Ten minutes for five-year-olds, forty for teenagers – ‘The skeleton stays the same, but everything else moves.’"},{q:"Which audiences does Mira find most difficult?",o:["teenagers","adults at social events","young children"],a:1,x:"‘The most difficult are adults at parties… secretly checking their phones.’"},{q:"How has technology affected Mira's work?",o:["It has made listening to stories more special.","It has reduced the number of bookings.","It has made audiences less patient."],a:0,x:"‘sitting in a room and just listening to someone has become unusual, almost a treat’"},{q:"Why do teachers often book Mira?",o:["to help students improve their listening","to teach students about India","to give teachers a break"],a:0,x:"‘they want their students to practise listening’"},{q:"What advice does Mira give to new storytellers?",o:["to memorise the exact words","to imagine the story visually","to practise in front of a mirror"],a:1,x:"‘Don't learn the words. Learn the pictures.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.plistening.push({id:"pl4",practice:!0,title:"Practice set 4",topics:"Everyday situations · Training as an airline pilot · A memorable birthday · A video game sound designer",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a jacket he returned to a shop.",q:"Why did he return it?",o:["It was the wrong size.","It was damaged.","He found it cheaper elsewhere."],a:1,x:"‘one of the pockets was torn’. The size was ‘perfect’ and he didn't look for a cheaper one.",script:[["M","The size was perfect, which is rare for me, and I liked the colour. But when I got home and looked at it properly, one of the pockets was torn along the seam. I didn't want to wait for a replacement, so I just asked for my money back. They were very nice about it – no questions at all."]]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a holiday they're planning.",q:"What do they disagree about?",o:["how long to go for","where to stay","how to travel"],a:2,x:"She wants to fly, he wants the train – ‘it's half the fun’. They agree on a week and the flat.",script:[["F","So, a week in Barcelona, and we've agreed on that little flat near the beach."],["M","Yes. And I've looked at trains – we could go via Paris."],["F","Trains? It would take two days. Let's just fly."],["M","But the journey's half the fun!"],["F","Not for me, it isn't."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman leaving a message for a dentist.",q:"Why is she calling?",o:["to make an appointment","to ask about a bill","to cancel an appointment"],a:0,x:"‘I'd like to book a check-up… any time next week’. She paid her last bill and has no appointment to cancel.",script:[["F","Hello, this is Sarah Kim. I'd like to book a check-up, please – it's been over a year, I'm afraid. Any time next week is fine, except Wednesday morning. I've paid the bill from my last visit, by the way, in case there was any confusion about that. Please call me back on this number. Thanks."]]},{ctx:"You hear a teenager talking about a summer camp.",q:"How did he feel at the end of it?",o:["glad to go home","sad to leave his new friends","tired of the activities"],a:1,x:"‘On the last day nobody wanted to leave… we've got a group chat now’. He loved the activities.",script:[["M","I didn't want to go at first – two weeks with people I didn't know. But the activities were amazing, especially the kayaking. And on the last day nobody wanted to leave. A few of us were actually crying on the bus, which was embarrassing. We've got a group chat now, and we're planning to meet up in the holidays."]]},{ctx:"You hear a museum guide speaking to visitors.",q:"What is she doing?",o:["describing a famous painting","explaining the museum's rules","introducing a new exhibition"],a:2,x:"‘This new exhibition, which opened last week, brings together…’ – the whole message introduces it.",script:[["F","Welcome to the east wing. This new exhibition, which opened just last week, brings together photographs of the city taken over the last hundred years. Many of them have never been shown in public before. Some come from professional photographers, but many were sent in by local families. Do take your time – there's a lot to see."]]},{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a running app.",q:"What does he like most about it?",o:["It plans his routes.","It encourages him to keep going.","It connects him with other runners."],a:1,x:"‘the voice that tells you you're nearly there… keeps me going when I want to stop’. He plans his own routes and doesn't use the social part.",script:[["M","I don't use the route planner – I like choosing my own way. And there's a social bit where you can follow other runners, but that's not for me. What I love is the voice that tells you you're nearly there, and how much faster you are than last time. It sounds silly, but it keeps me going when I want to stop."]]},{ctx:"You hear two students talking about a group project.",q:"What is the woman worried about?",o:["One member isn't doing their share.","The deadline is too soon.","The topic is too difficult."],a:0,x:"‘Ollie hasn't done anything yet’. She says the deadline is ‘fine’ and the topic ‘interesting’.",script:[["M","So, how's the project going?"],["F","The topic's interesting, and the deadline's fine – we've got three weeks. The problem is Ollie. He hasn't done anything yet, and he doesn't answer messages."],["M","Have you spoken to the teacher?"],["F","Not yet. I don't want to get him into trouble."]]},{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a book she has read.",q:"What does she say about the main character?",o:["She was unrealistic.","She was easy to identify with.","She changed too little."],a:1,x:"‘I recognised myself in almost everything she did’. She praises the character, not criticising realism or change.",script:[["F","The plot was a bit slow in the middle, I'll admit, but I loved the main character. She's a bit disorganised, always late, always saying the wrong thing, and I recognised myself in almost everything she did. By the end she'd changed so much – grown up, really. I didn't want to say goodbye to her."]]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Zara Hussain talking about her training to become an airline pilot.",script:[["F",`Good afternoon. I'm Zara Hussain, and eighteen months ago I started training to become an airline pilot. Before that I was a primary school teacher, which surprises most people. I'd always loved planes, but I didn't think someone like me could fly them until a friend's brother, who's a captain, told me otherwise.

The training is long. The first part is all theory – fourteen exams on subjects like weather, navigation and the law. I found meteorology the hardest, which is ironic, because the weather is something I'd talked about with children every single day.

After the exams, you start flying small planes. My first solo flight was over farmland near the coast. My instructor got out of the plane, closed the door and said, ‘See you in twenty minutes.’ I've never concentrated so hard in my life.

A surprising amount of the training happens in a simulator, which is an exact copy of a real cockpit that moves. In the simulator, instructors can create emergencies that would be far too dangerous to practise in the air, such as an engine failure on take-off.

People think pilots spend most of their time flying the plane by hand. In fact, on a long flight the autopilot does most of the work. The pilot's job is to monitor everything and make decisions. The skill that matters most isn't flying – it's communication with the rest of the crew.

The training isn't cheap. Some airlines now pay for it in return for a contract, which is how I'm able to do it. Otherwise it can cost as much as a house.

The thing I miss about teaching is the children's questions. But I'll get my first airline job next spring, and I've already been invited back to my old school to give a talk.

If you're thinking about becoming a pilot, my advice is to book a trial lesson. It costs about the same as a nice dinner, and after one hour you'll know whether this is for you.`]],items:[{s:"Before training, Zara worked as a {g}.",a:["primary school teacher","teacher","school teacher"],x:"‘Before that I was a primary school teacher’"},{s:"Zara was encouraged by a friend's brother, who is a {g}.",a:["captain","pilot"],x:"‘a friend's brother, who's a captain’"},{s:"The theory part of the training includes {g} exams.",a:["fourteen","14"],x:"‘fourteen exams on subjects like weather, navigation and the law’"},{s:"Zara found {g} the most difficult subject.",a:["meteorology","the weather","weather"],x:"‘I found meteorology the hardest’"},{s:"Zara's first solo flight was over {g} near the coast.",a:["farmland"],x:"‘My first solo flight was over farmland near the coast.’"},{s:"In the simulator, trainees practise dealing with {g}.",a:["emergencies","emergency situations"],x:"‘instructors can create emergencies that would be far too dangerous to practise in the air’"},{s:"Zara says the most important skill for a pilot is {g}.",a:["communication"],x:"‘The skill that matters most isn't flying – it's communication’"},{s:"Without help from an airline, the training can cost as much as a {g}.",a:["house"],x:"‘it can cost as much as a house’"},{s:"What Zara misses about her old job is the children's {g}.",a:["questions"],x:"‘The thing I miss about teaching is the children's questions.’"},{s:"Zara recommends that people book a {g} first.",a:["trial lesson"],x:"‘my advice is to book a trial lesson’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about a memorable birthday.",prompt:"For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker says about the birthday. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.",options:["Nobody remembered it at first.","I spent it somewhere unusual.","A present made me cry.","It didn't go as planned, but it was still special.","I celebrated with people I'd just met.","I didn't want any fuss.","I was given a surprise trip.","It was the first birthday I spent alone."],speakers:[{a:2,x:"C: reading the book of letters, ‘I cried for an hour’. There was a party, and nothing suggests she didn't want one (F).",script:[["F","For my fiftieth, my children secretly wrote to everyone I'd ever worked with and asked them for a letter. They put them all into a book. I sat down to read it after the party and I cried for an hour – people remembered things I'd completely forgotten. It's the most precious thing I own."]]},{a:3,x:"D: ‘the restaurant had double-booked… we ended up eating chips on the beach – the best birthday ever’.",script:[["M","I'd booked a lovely restaurant for my twenty-first, and when we arrived they'd given our table to someone else. Twelve of us, standing on the pavement. So we bought chips and sat on the beach and watched the sun go down. Honestly, it was the best birthday I've ever had. Nobody remembers the restaurants."]]},{a:4,x:"E: ‘I'd only arrived in the hostel that morning… by midnight the whole hostel was singing to me’. She wasn't alone (H).",script:[["F","I turned twenty in a hostel in New Zealand. I'd only arrived that morning and didn't know a soul, and I thought it would be the first birthday I'd ever spent alone. Then I mentioned it to the girl in the next bed, and by midnight the whole hostel was singing to me – people from about ten countries."]]},{a:0,x:"A: ‘my whole family forgot… until my sister rang at nine in the evening’. It went well in the end, but it wasn't a change of plan (D).",script:[["M","My whole family forgot my birthday last year. My wife was away for work, the kids were busy with exams, and my parents got the date mixed up. I was quite upset, honestly. Then at nine in the evening my sister rang, horrified, and drove over with a cake from the petrol station. We still laugh about it."]]},{a:1,x:"B: ‘I spent my thirtieth halfway up a mountain in a tent’. She chose it herself – it wasn't a surprise trip (G).",script:[["F","I spent my thirtieth halfway up a mountain in a tent, in a snowstorm. My choice, before you ask – I'd always wanted to climb that mountain, and the only free week was my birthday week. My friend had carried a tiny cake all the way up, slightly squashed. We ate it with gloves on."]]}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a man called Leon Marsh, who designs sound effects for video games.",script:[["F","My guest is Leon Marsh, who creates the sounds for video games. Leon, what does a sound designer actually do?"],["M","Everything you hear in a game that isn't music or speech – footsteps, doors, weather, monsters. Players rarely notice good sound, but they notice immediately when it's missing. Turn the sound off in your favourite game and see how long you keep playing."],["F","Where do the sounds come from?"],["M","Some I record myself, and some I create on a computer, but most are a mixture. For a dragon's roar in my last game, I combined a recording of a lion, my own voice through a pipe, and the sound of a heavy door opening very slowly. People are always disappointed when I tell them that."],["F","How did you get into the industry?"],["M","I was a drummer in bands for years, earning almost nothing. A friend who worked at a games company needed someone to record some sounds for a weekend, and I said yes because I needed the money. I enjoyed it far more than I expected, and they offered me a job."],["F","What's the most challenging part of the job?"],["M","Variety. In a film, a sound plays once. In a game, a player might hear the same footstep ten thousand times. If it's exactly the same every time, it becomes really annoying, so I record dozens of slightly different versions, and the game chooses one at random."],["F","Do you play the games you work on?"],["M","Endlessly – but not for fun, at least not until they're finished. I'm listening all the time. My partner says I'm the only person who plays games with my eyes closed."],["F","What advice would you give to young people who want to do this?"],["M","Carry a recorder everywhere – your phone is fine. Record anything interesting: rain on a car roof, a squeaky gate, a crowd in a station. Build your own library of sounds. When you apply for a job, that collection will say more about you than any qualification."]],qs:[{q:"What does Leon say about sound in games?",o:["Players notice it most when it isn't there.","It is less important than the music.","Most players turn it off."],a:0,x:"‘they notice immediately when it's missing’"},{q:"How did Leon make the dragon's roar?",o:["He recorded a real animal only.","He combined several different sounds.","He created it entirely on a computer."],a:1,x:"A lion, his own voice through a pipe and a heavy door."},{q:"Why did Leon first agree to work for a games company?",o:["He had always wanted to work in games.","He needed the money.","His band had broken up."],a:1,x:"‘I said yes because I needed the money’"},{q:"What is the main challenge of creating sounds for games?",o:["Players hear the same sounds many times.","Games have very short deadlines.","Sounds must match the music."],a:0,x:"‘a player might hear the same footstep ten thousand times’"},{q:"How does Leon solve this problem?",o:["He uses fewer sounds.","He makes sounds quieter.","He records many variations of each sound."],a:2,x:"‘I record dozens of slightly different versions, and the game chooses one at random’"},{q:"What does Leon's partner's comment show?",o:["He doesn't enjoy playing games.","He concentrates on listening while playing.","He plays games too often."],a:1,x:"‘plays games with my eyes closed’ – he's listening, not watching."},{q:"What advice does Leon give to young people?",o:["to get a qualification in sound design","to collect recordings of everyday sounds","to learn to play a musical instrument"],a:1,x:"‘Build your own library of sounds… that collection will say more about you than any qualification.’"}]}}});
